            "---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "## Step 17.3 — Quantitative Comparison of Detector Responses\n",
            "\n",
            "### Objective\n",
            "Quantify the absolute and percentage changes in Euclidean and Mahalanobis detector scores under low- and high-illumination conditions relative to the original illumination baseline.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Methodology\n",
            "1. Use the original illumination scores as the reference baseline.\n",
            "2. Calculate absolute score changes for both transformed conditions.\n",
            "3. Calculate percentage changes relative to the original scores.\n",
            "4. Compare the response magnitude of both detectors independently.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Research Purpose\n",
            "Quantify the relative sensitivity of the two embedding-level detectors to controlled illumination transformations.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Scientific Limitation\n",
            "The comparison uses three aggregate detector scores per method. Percentage changes do not establish statistical significance, detection accuracy, or false-alarm control.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Status\n",
            "Successfully completed. Quantitative detector response changes were calculated by comparing the original-condition baseline scores with the low- and high-illumination scores.\n",
            "\n",
            "Under low illumination, the Euclidean score increased from 0.053114 to 0.094771, representing an absolute change of 0.041657 (78.430107%). The Mahalanobis score increased from 0.797920 to 1.735851, representing an absolute change of 0.937931 (117.546901%).\n",
            "\n",
            "Under high illumination, the Euclidean score increased from 0.053114 to 0.082996, representing an absolute change of 0.029882 (56.260796%). The Mahalanobis score increased from 0.797920 to 1.871540, representing an absolute change of 1.073620 (134.552237%).\n",
            "\n",
            "Numerical validation confirmed that all comparison values were finite.\n",
            "\n",
            "Both detectors showed increased embedding-level scores under the controlled illumination transformations. The magnitude of the response differed between detectors and illumination conditions. These changes indicate sensitivity to the applied transformations, not a direct measurement of biometric recognition error or accuracy.\n",
            "\n",
