            "---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "## Step 17.4 — Identity-Level Detector Response Analysis\n",
            "\n",
            "### Objective\n",
            "Evaluate identity-level variations in Euclidean and Mahalanobis embedding displacement under controlled illumination transformations.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Methodology\n",
            "1. Group paired original, low-illumination and high-illumination embeddings by identity.\n",
            "2. Calculate identity-level mean embeddings for each condition.\n",
            "3. Calculate Euclidean displacement between original and transformed identity centroids.\n",
            "4. Calculate regularised Mahalanobis displacement using the identity-balanced reference covariance.\n",
            "5. Compare low- and high-illumination responses across identities.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Research Purpose\n",
            "Determine whether illumination-induced representation changes are consistent across identities or exhibit identity-dependent variation.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Scientific Limitation\n",
            "Identity-level results are based on a limited evaluation cohort of 13 identities. They should not be interpreted as population-level biometric performance or fairness evidence.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Status\n",
            "Successfully completed. Identity-level detector responses were recalculated using the correct 229-image evaluation cohort across 13 identities.\n",
            "\n",
            "For Euclidean displacement, the mean identity-level score was 0.126002 under low illumination and 0.156053 under high illumination. The corresponding medians were 0.116416 and 0.149594.\n",
            "\n",
            "For Mahalanobis displacement, the mean identity-level score was 2.389304 under low illumination and 3.111986 under high illumination. The corresponding medians were 2.279956 and 2.937519.\n",
            "\n",
            "All 13 identities were evaluated, with identity-specific sample counts ranging from 5 to 126 images. All Euclidean and Mahalanobis scores passed numerical validation.\n",
            "\n",
            "The results indicate that detector responses vary across identities and illumination conditions. High illumination produced higher mean identity-level displacement for both detectors. However, the unequal identity sample counts and limited cohort size must be considered when interpreting these findings. These measurements do not establish biometric recognition accuracy or fairness.\n",
