            "---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "## Step 17.9 — Research Findings and Scientific Interpretation\n",
            "\n",
            "### 1. Overall Experimental Findings\n",
            "\n",
            "The controlled illumination evaluation demonstrated measurable changes in aggregate embedding-level Euclidean and Mahalanobis detector responses across original, low-illumination and high-illumination conditions.\n",
            "\n",
            "Both detectors produced higher aggregate scores under transformed illumination compared with the original condition.\n",
            "\n",
            "### 2. Euclidean Detector Findings\n",
            "\n",
            "The Euclidean detector recorded an aggregate score of 0.053114 under original illumination, 0.094771 under low illumination and 0.082996 under high illumination.\n",
            "\n",
            "The corresponding increases relative to the original condition were:\n",
            "\n",
            "- Low illumination: 78.43%.\n",
            "- High illumination: 56.26%.\n",
            "\n",
            "The identity-level analysis showed that 8 of the 13 identities exhibited higher Euclidean displacement under high illumination compared with low illumination.\n",
            "\n",
            "The Wilcoxon signed-rank test did not identify a statistically significant paired difference (p = 0.375732).\n",
            "\n",
            "### 3. Mahalanobis Detector Findings\n",
            "\n",
            "The Mahalanobis detector recorded an aggregate score of 0.797920 under original illumination, 1.735851 under low illumination and 1.871540 under high illumination.\n",
            "\n",
            "The corresponding increases relative to the original condition were:\n",
            "\n",
            "- Low illumination: 117.55%.\n",
            "- High illumination: 134.55%.\n",
            "\n",
            "The identity-level analysis showed that 9 of the 13 identities exhibited higher Mahalanobis displacement under high illumination compared with low illumination.\n",
            "\n",
            "The Wilcoxon signed-rank test returned p = 0.057373. This did not reach the conventional 5% significance threshold.\n",
            "\n",
            "### 4. Identity-Level Variation\n",
            "\n",
            "The identity-level analysis demonstrated that detector responses were not uniform across the 13 evaluation identities.\n",
            "\n",
            "Some identities exhibited greater displacement under low illumination, whereas others exhibited greater displacement under high illumination.\n",
            "\n",
            "This indicates identity-dependent variation in embedding displacement under the controlled transformations.\n",
            "\n",
            "### 5. Effect Size and Uncertainty Findings\n",
            "\n",
            "The paired standardised effect sizes were:\n",
            "\n",
            "- Euclidean Cohen's dz: 0.296882.\n",
            "- Mahalanobis Cohen's dz: 0.596839.\n",
            "\n",
            "The corresponding rank-biserial correlations were:\n",
            "\n",
            "- Euclidean: 0.296703.\n",
            "- Mahalanobis: 0.604396.\n",
            "\n",
            "The mean bootstrap confidence interval for Euclidean differences included zero, whereas the Mahalanobis mean bootstrap confidence interval was positive.\n",
            "\n",
            "However, the Mahalanobis median bootstrap confidence interval included zero.\n",
            "\n",
            "These results indicate greater estimated high-versus-low illumination response magnitude for Mahalanobis distance, while uncertainty remains regarding the consistency of this difference.\n",
            "\n",
            "### 6. Comparative Scientific Interpretation\n",
            "\n",
            "The two detectors demonstrated different response patterns under the controlled illumination transformations.\n",
            "\n",
            "Euclidean distance exhibited its largest aggregate response under low illumination, whereas Mahalanobis distance exhibited its largest aggregate response under high illumination.\n",
            "\n",
            "The identity-level findings further indicate that these response patterns vary across identities.\n",
            "\n",
            "Mahalanobis distance produced larger estimated paired effect sizes in this evaluation. However, the available statistical evidence does not establish its general superiority over Euclidean distance.\n",
            "\n",
            "### 7. Scientific Limitations\n",
            "\n",
            "The following limitations apply to the findings:\n",
            "\n",
            "1. The evaluation includes only 13 identities.\n",
            "2. Identity-level sample counts are unequal.\n",
            "3. The aggregate comparison contains only three illumination-condition scores per detector.\n",
            "4. The Wilcoxon tests did not demonstrate statistically significant paired differences at the 5% level.\n",
            "5. Bootstrap confidence intervals are exploratory and based on only 13 identity-level observations.\n",
            "6. The experiment does not establish actual biometric recognition accuracy degradation.\n",
            "7. The experiment does not establish demographic fairness or population-level generalisation.\n",
            "8. The results do not establish operational drift-detection accuracy or false-alarm control.\n",
            "\n",
            "### 8. Overall Research Conclusion\n",
            "\n",
            "The experiment demonstrates that controlled illumination transformations are associated with measurable embedding-level displacement under both Euclidean and Mahalanobis distance measures.\n",
            "\n",
            "The detectors exhibit different aggregate response patterns and identity-dependent variations.\n",
            "\n",
            "Mahalanobis distance demonstrates a larger estimated response magnitude in the paired high-versus-low illumination comparison. Nevertheless, the small evaluation cohort and exploratory statistical evidence prevent a definitive conclusion regarding detector superiority.\n",
            "\n",
            "The findings provide preliminary evidence supporting further investigation of identity-dependent embedding displacement and detector sensitivity under controlled illumination changes.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Status\n",
            "\n",
            "Successfully completed. Aggregate detector responses, identity-level comparisons, paired Wilcoxon tests, effect sizes and bootstrap confidence intervals were integrated and validated using the corrected 229-image evaluation cohort across 13 identities. Both paired Wilcoxon tests were statistically significant at the nominal 5% level (Euclidean p = 0.021484; Mahalanobis p = 0.002441). Findings and scientific limitations were documented. Statistical conclusions remain exploratory because of the limited identity cohort and absence of multiple-comparison correction.\n",
            "\n",
            "===========================================================================\n",
            "CELL INDEX: 111\n",
