      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Experiment 20 — Step 3.3.6\n",
        "## Introduction\n",
        "### 1. Background: Face Recognition and Deep Face Embeddings\n",
        "\n",
        "Face recognition systems increasingly use deep learning models to represent facial images as numerical feature vectors, commonly referred to as face embeddings. These representations encode facial characteristics in a high-dimensional feature space, where distances or similarities between embeddings can be used to examine relationships between facial images.\n",
        "\n",
        "The geometry of this embedding space is relevant to face-recognition research because changes in an input image may be reflected in the position of its corresponding representation. Consequently, comparing embeddings can provide a way to examine how image variations are associated with changes in learned facial representations.\n",
        "\n",
        "A particularly important source of image variation is illumination. Changes in lighting direction and intensity can alter the appearance of facial regions, producing differences between images of the same individual. The effects of illumination on face recognition have been investigated in earlier research, including work examining changes in illumination direction (Moses, Adini, and Ullman, 1994).\n",
        "\n",
        "In this context, examining reference-relative embedding displacement provides an empirical way to describe how the representations of different captures of an individual vary relative to a selected reference capture. The present study applies this approach to a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model and examines Euclidean displacement and cosine distance across groups of illumination conditions.\n",
        "\n",
        "This investigation focuses on the geometry of the resulting embeddings. It does not, by itself, establish whether a face-recognition system correctly identifies individuals under those conditions."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "P9r9KKOn-MmD"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "from pathlib import Path\n",
        "import json\n",
        "from datetime import datetime, timezone\n",
        "\n",
        "BASE = Path(\"/content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results\")\n",
        "STEP_DIR = BASE / \"step_3_3\"\n",
        "STEP_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "OUTPUT_FILE = STEP_DIR / (\n",
        "    \"experiment20_step336_introduction_section1_background.json\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "section = {\n",
        "    \"section_number\": \"3.3.6.1\",\n",
        "    \"heading\": \"Background: Face Recognition and Deep Face Embeddings\",\n",
        "    \"draft\": \"\"\"Face recognition systems increasingly use deep learning models to represent facial images as numerical feature vectors, commonly referred to as face embeddings. These representations encode facial characteristics in a high-dimensional feature space, where distances or similarities between embeddings can be used to examine relationships between facial images.\n",
        "\n",
        "The geometry of this embedding space is relevant to face-recognition research because changes in an input image may be reflected in the position of its corresponding representation. Consequently, comparing embeddings can provide a way to examine how image variations are associated with changes in learned facial representations.\n",
        "\n",
        "A particularly important source of image variation is illumination. Changes in lighting direction and intensity can alter the appearance of facial regions, producing differences between images of the same individual. The effects of illumination on face recognition have been investigated in earlier research, including work examining changes in illumination direction (Moses, Adini, and Ullman, 1994).\n",
        "\n",
        "In this context, examining reference-relative embedding displacement provides an empirical way to describe how the representations of different captures of an individual vary relative to a selected reference capture. The present study applies this approach to a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model and examines Euclidean displacement and cosine distance across groups of illumination conditions.\n",
        "\n",
        "This investigation focuses on the geometry of the resulting embeddings. It does not, by itself, establish whether a face-recognition system correctly identifies individuals under those conditions.\"\"\",\n",
        "    \"supporting_reference\": {\n",
        "        \"authors\": \"Moses, Adini, and Ullman\",\n",
        "        \"year\": 1994,\n",
        "        \"title\": \"Face Recognition: The Problem of Compensating for Changes in Illumination Direction\",\n",
        "        \"doi\": \"10.1007/3-540-57956-7_33\",\n",
        "        \"verification_status\": \"Present in preliminary literature baseline\"\n",
        "    },\n",
        "    \"scope_checks\": {\n",
        "        \"recognition_accuracy_claimed\": False,\n",
        "        \"operational_robustness_claimed\": False,\n",
        "        \"embedding_displacement_focus\": True,\n",
        "        \"fixed_pretrained_model_identified\": True\n",
        "    },\n",
        "    \"status\": \"FIRST_BACKGROUND_SECTION_DRAFT_PREPARED\",\n",
        "    \"timestamp_utc\": datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
