            "FILE: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/step_3_3/experiment20_step3362_introduction_illumination_context.json\n",
            "MATCHES: 1\n",
            "\n",
            "4:   \"step\": \"3.3.6.2\",\n",
            "5:   \"heading\": \"Illumination Variation and Its Effect on Facial Representations\",\n",
            "6:   \"draft\": \"Illumination variation is a longstanding challenge in face-recognition research. Changes in the direction of incident light can produce substantial differences in facial appearance, affecting the distribution of shadows, highlights, and visible facial details. These variations can occur even when the images represent the same individual. Earlier research by Moses, Adini, and Ullman (1994) specifically examined the problem of compensating for changes in illumination direction in face recognition.\\n\\nSubsequent research has investigated the relationship between illumination and facial appearance using different analytical approaches. Beveridge et al. (2009) examined subject illumination spaces and the role of principal angles in distinguishing them. Other studies have explored illumination preprocessing and methods intended to reduce the effects of lighting variation on face recognition (Han et al., 2013; Ochoa-Villegas et al., 2015). Research using Yale B datasets has also considered illumination conditions according to the angular position of light sources, demonstrating the relevance of illumination geometry in controlled face-image analysis.\\n\\nDeep face-recognition models represent facial images through learned feature embeddings. Changes in facial appearance may therefore be accompanied by changes in embedding-space position. However, the magnitude and consistency of such changes depend on the model, the input images, and the conditions under which the representations are evaluated. An observed embedding displacement should consequently be interpreted as a change in representation rather than direct evidence of incorrect identity recognition.\\n\\nIn the present study, illumination conditions are grouped according to their angular geometry relative to a common reference condition. Three groups are considered: near-frontal illumination, oblique illumination, and large-angular-deviation illumination. These categories provide a structured basis for comparing reference-relative embedding displacement across different lighting directions. They represent angular geometry rather than illumination intensity.\\n\\nThis distinction is important because the experiment uses different captures of the same individuals under different illumination conditions. The images are identity-matched observations, not exact transformations of identical source images. Therefore, the resulting differences in embedding space are examined as reference-relative associations with illumination geometry, rather than as effects attributable exclusively to illumination.\",\n",
            "7:   \"word_count\": 328,\n",
            "8:   \"supporting_references\": [\n",
            "\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "FILE: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/step_3_3/step_337/experiment20_consolidated_introduction.md\n",
            "MATCHES: 2\n",
            "\n",
            "18: Illumination variation is a longstanding challenge in face-recognition research. Changes in the direction of incident light can produce substantial differences in facial appearance, affecting the distribution of shadows, highlights, and visible facial details. These variations can occur even when the images represent the same individual. Earlier research by Moses, Adini, and Ullman (1994) specifically examined the problem of compensating for changes in illumination direction in face recognition.\n",
            "19: \n",
            "20: Subsequent research has investigated the relationship between illumination and facial appearance using different analytical approaches. Beveridge et al. (2009) examined subject illumination spaces and the role of principal angles in distinguishing them. Other studies have explored illumination preprocessing and methods intended to reduce the effects of lighting variation on face recognition (Han et al., 2013; Ochoa-Villegas et al., 2015). Research using Yale B datasets has also considered illumination conditions according to the angular position of light sources, demonstrating the relevance of illumination geometry in controlled face-image analysis.\n",
            "21: \n",
            "22: Deep face-recognition models represent facial images through learned feature embeddings. Changes in facial appearance may therefore be accompanied by changes in embedding-space position. However, the magnitude and consistency of such changes depend on the model, the input images, and the conditions under which the representations are evaluated. An observed embedding displacement should consequently be interpreted as a change in representation rather than direct evidence of incorrect identity recognition.\n",
            "\n",
            "22: Deep face-recognition models represent facial images through learned feature embeddings. Changes in facial appearance may therefore be accompanied by changes in embedding-space position. However, the magnitude and consistency of such changes depend on the model, the input images, and the conditions under which the representations are evaluated. An observed embedding displacement should consequently be interpreted as a change in representation rather than direct evidence of incorrect identity recognition.\n",
