            "FILE: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/step_3_3/step_3391/experiment20_methodology_3_1_research_design.md\n",
            "MATCHES: 1\n",
            "\n",
            "5: The evaluation used the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset, comprising 2,414 standard-illumination images from 38 identities. A common-condition selection procedure identified 57 illumination conditions represented across all 38 identities, yielding 2,166 identity-condition observations. One observation per identity corresponded to the designated reference condition, A+000E+00. These 38 reference observations were used to establish the identity-specific reference embeddings. After excluding the reference condition, the primary evaluation comprised 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 illumination conditions.\n",
            "6: \n",
            "7: The primary unit of observation was an identity-condition pair, while identity served as the repeated-measures unit for group-level statistical comparisons. This structure allowed displacement measurements from the same 38 identities to be examined across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The observations represent identity-condition-matched captures under different illumination conditions; they are not pairs produced by applying controlled transformations to identical source images.\n",
            "8: \n",
            "9: The analysis was conducted using 512-dimensional embeddings generated by a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model with VGGFace2 weights. Each evaluation embedding was compared with the corresponding identity's reference embedding. Euclidean displacement and cosine distance were then summarised across illumination geometry groups and examined using identity-level repeated-measures statistical procedures.\n",
            "\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "FILE: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/step_3_3/step_3391/experiment20_step3391_research_design.json\n",
            "MATCHES: 1\n",
            "\n",
            "6:   \"created_utc\": \"2026-11-11T10:33:04.260935+00:00\",\n",
            "7:   \"research_design\": \"Descriptive empirical analysis\",\n",
            "8:   \"draft_text\": \"Experiment 20 employed a descriptive empirical design to examine reference-relative displacement in deep face-recognition embeddings across illumination geometry conditions. The analysis focused on the extent to which the embedding representation of an identity's facial image differed from that identity's embedding under a designated reference illumination condition. Rather than developing or evaluating an operational drift-detection algorithm, the experiment characterised these differences using two distance measures: Euclidean displacement and cosine distance.\\n\\nThe evaluation used the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset, comprising 2,414 standard-illumination images from 38 identities. A common-condition selection procedure identified 57 illumination conditions represented across all 38 identities, yielding 2,166 identity-condition observations. One observation per identity corresponded to the designated reference condition, A+000E+00. These 38 reference observations were used to establish the identity-specific reference embeddings. After excluding the reference condition, the primary evaluation comprised 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 illumination conditions.\\n\\nThe primary unit of observation was an identity-condition pair, while identity served as the repeated-measures unit for group-level statistical comparisons. This structure allowed displacement measurements from the same 38 identities to be examined across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The observations represent identity-condition-matched captures under different illumination conditions; they are not pairs produced by applying controlled transformations to identical source images.\\n\\nThe analysis was conducted using 512-dimensional embeddings generated by a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model with VGGFace2 weights. Each evaluation embedding was compared with the corresponding identity's reference embedding. Euclidean displacement and cosine distance were then summarised across illumination geometry groups and examined using identity-level repeated-measures statistical procedures.\\n\\nThe study was intentionally restricted to descriptive embedding-space analysis. It did not estimate calibrated detection thresholds, operational false-alarm rates, or recognition accuracy. It also did not evaluate an independent face-recognition architecture. Consequently, the results characterise reference-relative embedding displacement within the selected dataset, preprocessing procedure, and pretrained model; they do not establish that illumination alone caused the observed differences or that the findings generalise to other recognition systems.\",\n",
            "9:   \"verified_protocol_facts\": {\n",
            "10:     \"dataset\": \"Cropped Extended Yale B\",\n",
            "\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "FILE: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/step_3_3/step_3393/experiment20_methodology_3_3_subject_condition_selection.md\n",
            "MATCHES: 2\n",
            "\n",
            "7: The condition A+000E+00 was designated as the reference condition. One reference observation was available for each of the 38 identities, providing an identity-specific reference embedding. The reference observations were retained for calculating reference-relative distances but excluded from the non-reference evaluation set. Consequently, the primary evaluation comprised the remaining 56 common conditions and 2,128 identity-condition observations.\n",
            "8: \n",
            "9: The 56 non-reference conditions were assigned to three illumination geometry groups using the frozen condition-grouping manifest: near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation. The corresponding evaluation counts were 17, 11, and 28 conditions, respectively, representing 646, 418, and 1,064 observations. The reference condition belonged to the near-frontal group but was excluded from these evaluation counts.\n",
            "10: \n",
            "11: The geometry groups represent the angular deviation of the illumination direction relative to the camera axis, as defined by the study's condition-grouping procedure. They do not represent directly measured illumination intensity. The same 38 identities were represented in each of the three evaluation groups, enabling identity-level repeated-measures comparisons. The resulting observations are identity-condition-matched captures under different lighting conditions, not exact image-level pairs generated from a single source image.\n",
            "\n",
            "9: The 56 non-reference conditions were assigned to three illumination geometry groups using the frozen condition-grouping manifest: near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation. The corresponding evaluation counts were 17, 11, and 28 conditions, respectively, representing 646, 418, and 1,064 observations. The reference condition belonged to the near-frontal group but was excluded from these evaluation counts.\n",
            "10: \n",
            "11: The geometry groups represent the angular deviation of the illumination direction relative to the camera axis, as defined by the study's condition-grouping procedure. They do not represent directly measured illumination intensity. The same 38 identities were represented in each of the three evaluation groups, enabling identity-level repeated-measures comparisons. The resulting observations are identity-condition-matched captures under different lighting conditions, not exact image-level pairs generated from a single source image.\n",
            "\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "FILE: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/step_3_3/step_3393/experiment20_step3393_subject_condition_selection.json\n",
            "MATCHES: 4\n",
            "\n",
            "5:   \"subsection\": \"3.3 Subject Selection and Illumination-Condition Coverage\",\n",
            "6:   \"created_utc\": \"2026-11-11T10:34:52.039353+00:00\",\n",
            "7:   \"draft_text\": \"The subject cohort comprised 38 identities represented in the Cropped Extended Yale B frontal-pose illumination subset. The standard-illumination image collection contained 64 distinct illumination conditions across the cohort; however, not every condition was available for every identity. Seven identities had incomplete condition coverage. To preserve a consistent identity set across the primary condition-based comparisons, the analysis retained only conditions represented for all 38 identities.\\n\\nThis complete-case condition selection produced 57 common illumination conditions and 2,166 identity-condition observations, corresponding to 38 observations for each condition. The selection was based on shared condition availability rather than on embedding displacement values or statistical outcomes. Conditions not represented across all 38 identities were excluded from analyses requiring complete identity-condition coverage.\\n\\nThe condition A+000E+00 was designated as the reference condition. One reference observation was available for each of the 38 identities, providing an identity-specific reference embedding. The reference observations were retained for calculating reference-relative distances but excluded from the non-reference evaluation set. Consequently, the primary evaluation comprised the remaining 56 common conditions and 2,128 identity-condition observations.\\n\\nThe 56 non-reference conditions were assigned to three illumination geometry groups using the frozen condition-grouping manifest: near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation. The corresponding evaluation counts were 17, 11, and 28 conditions, respectively, representing 646, 418, and 1,064 observations. The reference condition belonged to the near-frontal group but was excluded from these evaluation counts.\\n\\nThe geometry groups represent the angular deviation of the illumination direction relative to the camera axis, as defined by the study's condition-grouping procedure. They do not represent directly measured illumination intensity. The same 38 identities were represented in each of the three evaluation groups, enabling identity-level repeated-measures comparisons. The resulting observations are identity-condition-matched captures under different lighting conditions, not exact image-level pairs generated from a single source image.\",\n",
            "8:   \"verified_selection_facts\": {\n",
            "9:     \"identity_count\": 38,\n",
            "\n",
            "26:         \"observations\": 418\n",
            "27:       },\n",
            "28:       \"large_angular_deviation\": {\n",
            "29:         \"conditions\": 28,\n",
            "30:         \"observations\": 1064\n",
            "\n",
            "31:       }\n",
            "32:     },\n",
            "33:     \"grouping_interpretation\": \"Angular deviation of illumination direction, not measured intensity\"\n",
            "34:   },\n",
            "35:   \"word_count\": 296,\n",
            "\n",
            "61:     \"Common-condition selection maintains complete identity coverage.\",\n",
            "62:     \"Reference observations are used for distance calculation but excluded from non-reference evaluation counts.\",\n",
            "63:     \"Geometry groups describe angular deviation, not measured illumination intensity.\"\n",
            "64:   ]\n",
