          "text": [
            "====================================================================================================\n",
            "EXPERIMENT 20 — STEP 3.3.17 ORIGINAL HOLM IMPLEMENTATION TRACE\n",
            "====================================================================================================\n",
            "\n",
            "1. SEARCH INVENTORY\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "Project root: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results\n",
            "Files scanned: 25\n",
            "Files with relevant matches: 9\n",
            "Files skipped or unreadable: 0\n",
            "\n",
            "2. RELEVANT SOURCE LOCATIONS\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "\n",
            "FILE: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/step_3_3/step_3399/experiment20_methodology_3_8_statistical_analysis.md\n",
            "SHA256: e9c897d23d77ff1273badf32bae4a77f1c7e95d6aa2728d230fc814a54371d04\n",
            "\n",
            "Match 1 | line 7 | terms: holm, wilcoxon\n",
            "     2: \n",
            "     3: Statistical comparisons were conducted using identity-level summaries of the reference-relative distances. For each identity, the mean Euclidean displacement and mean cosine distance were calculated separately within the near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular illumination geometry groups. This aggregation provided three repeated measurements for each metric from each of the 38 identities.\n",
            "     4: \n",
            "     5: Differences across the three illumination geometry groups were evaluated using the Friedman test, a non-parametric repeated-measures procedure. The test was applied separately to Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Kendall's coefficient of concordance (W) was reported as the corresponding effect-size measure.\n",
            "     6: \n",
            "     7: Pairwise differences between illumination geometry groups were examined using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the paired identity-level summaries. Three pairwise comparisons were conducted for each distance metric: near-frontal versus oblique, near-frontal versus large-angular, and oblique versus large-angular. Holm's step-down procedure was applied across the combined family of six pairwise tests, comprising the three Euclidean comparisons and the three cosine comparisons, to account for multiple testing.\n",
            "     8: \n",
            "     9: Bootstrap confidence intervals were also calculated for the identity-level mean differences in the pairwise comparisons. The identity served as the resampling unit, preserving the paired structure of the repeated-measures observations. The reported intervals were 95% confidence intervals.\n",
            "    10: \n",
            "    11: An exploratory comparison between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions was additionally conducted using identity-level summaries. This comparison was interpreted cautiously because the two hemispheric categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions and observations and were associated with differences in angular geometry.\n",
            "    12: \n",
            "\n",
            "FILE: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/step_3_3/step_3311/experiment20_discussion_section_draft_v1.md\n",
            "SHA256: ed78d4dfcf079d369ad775115ea4abe0cbd8f3f469ca1c9cc9fa81846c666125\n",
            "\n",
            "Match 1 | line 21 | terms: holm\n",
            "    16: \n",
            "    17: ## 5.3 Identity-level consistency and statistical evidence\n",
            "    18: \n",
            "    19: The Friedman tests identified differences among the three illumination groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17). Kendall's W was 1.0 for both metrics. In this analysis, W = 1.0 indicates complete agreement in the ranking of the three group conditions across the evaluated identities. It should not be interpreted as perfect recognition performance, perfect embedding quality, or detector accuracy.\n",
            "    20: \n",
            "    21: All six pairwise identity-level comparisons remained statistically significant after Holm correction across the combined family of six comparisons. The consistency of the ordering across identities strengthens the descriptive evidence that the observed group pattern was not confined to a small subset of identities.\n",
            "    22: \n",
            "    23: However, the statistical evidence should be interpreted within the study design. The descriptive statistics are based on unequal numbers of image observations across the three illumination groups (646 near-frontal, 418 oblique, and 1,064 large-angular-deviation observations). In contrast, the repeated-measures analysis uses identity-level group means from the same 38 identities across all three groups. The inferential sample therefore consists of 38 matched identities, rather than the total number of image observations. This distinction is important when interpreting the descriptive and inferential results.\n",
            "    24: \n",
            "    25: ## 5.4 Relationship to previous illumination research\n",
            "    26: \n",
            "\n",
            "FILE: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/step_3_3/step_33915/experiment20_methodology_3_8_statistical_analysis_v2.md\n",
