            "    47: \n",
            "    48: \n",
            "    49: ## 4.5 Exploratory frontal and beyond-frontal comparison\n",
            "    50: \n",
            "    51: In the exploratory hemisphere analysis, mean Euclidean displacement was 0.706220 under frontal conditions and 1.287990 under beyond-frontal conditions. The mean paired difference (beyond-frontal minus frontal) was 0.581770 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.561986, 0.601954]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11).\n",
            "    52: \n",
            "    53: For cosine distance, the corresponding means were 0.295816 and 0.838624. The mean paired difference was 0.542807 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.516419, 0.568516]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11).\n",
            "    54: \n",
            "    55: This comparison is exploratory. The frontal and beyond-frontal categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions, and hemisphere membership is related to angular illumination geometry. Therefore, the observed differences should not be interpreted as an isolated causal effect of hemisphere direction.\n",
            "    56: \n",
            "\n",
            "Match 4 | line 53 | terms: holm\n",
            "    48: \n",
            "    49: ## 4.5 Exploratory frontal and beyond-frontal comparison\n",
            "    50: \n",
            "    51: In the exploratory hemisphere analysis, mean Euclidean displacement was 0.706220 under frontal conditions and 1.287990 under beyond-frontal conditions. The mean paired difference (beyond-frontal minus frontal) was 0.581770 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.561986, 0.601954]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11).\n",
            "    52: \n",
            "    53: For cosine distance, the corresponding means were 0.295816 and 0.838624. The mean paired difference was 0.542807 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.516419, 0.568516]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11).\n",
            "    54: \n",
            "    55: This comparison is exploratory. The frontal and beyond-frontal categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions, and hemisphere membership is related to angular illumination geometry. Therefore, the observed differences should not be interpreted as an isolated causal effect of hemisphere direction.\n",
            "    56: \n",
            "    57: \n",
            "    58: ## 4.6 Summary of findings\n",
            "\n",
            "Match 5 | line 60 | terms: holm\n",
            "    55: This comparison is exploratory. The frontal and beyond-frontal categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions, and hemisphere membership is related to angular illumination geometry. Therefore, the observed differences should not be interpreted as an isolated causal effect of hemisphere direction.\n",
            "    56: \n",
            "    57: \n",
            "    58: ## 4.6 Summary of findings\n",
            "    59: \n",
            "    60: The results show progressively greater reference-relative embedding displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The identity-level repeated-measures analysis identified statistically significant group differences for both distance measures, and all six pairwise comparisons remained significant following combined six-test Holm correction. The exploratory hemisphere analysis also showed higher mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions. These findings characterise embedding displacement within the evaluated dataset and fixed pretrained model; they do not establish recognition accuracy, operational drift-detection performance, or generalisation across architectures.\n",
            "\n",
            "FILE: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/step_3_3/step_3312/experiment20_integrated_manuscript_v1.md\n",
            "SHA256: 9c28be661b5136baecc9a583d1702a2d1f1a2d3045598722d3d55c649e5b8357\n",
            "\n",
            "Match 1 | line 26 | terms: holm\n",
            "    21: increased from 0.402 in near-frontal conditions to 0.709 in oblique\n",
            "    22: conditions and 1.118 in large-angular conditions. Corresponding mean\n",
            "    23: cosine distances increased from 0.087 to 0.262 and 0.649. Friedman\n",
            "    24: tests indicated significant differences across groups for both\n",
            "    25: metrics (chi-square = 76.0, p = 3.14e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0).\n",
            "    26: All six pairwise comparisons remained significant following Holm\n",
            "    27: correction. An exploratory hemisphere comparison also showed greater\n",
            "    28: mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions.\n",
            "    29: \n",
            "    30: These findings characterise reference-relative embedding displacement\n",
            "    31: within the evaluated dataset and model. However, the analysis uses\n",
            "\n",
            "Match 2 | line 203 | terms: holm, wilcoxon\n",
            "   198: \n",
            "   199: Statistical comparisons were conducted using identity-level summaries of the reference-relative distances. For each identity, the mean Euclidean displacement and mean cosine distance were calculated separately within the near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular illumination geometry groups. This aggregation provided three repeated measurements for each metric from each of the 38 identities.\n",
            "   200: \n",
            "   201: Differences across the three illumination geometry groups were evaluated using the Friedman test, a non-parametric repeated-measures procedure. The test was applied separately to Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Kendall's coefficient of concordance (W) was reported as the corresponding effect-size measure.\n",
            "   202: \n",
            "   203: Pairwise differences between illumination geometry groups were examined using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the paired identity-level summaries. Three pairwise comparisons were conducted for each distance metric: near-frontal versus oblique, near-frontal versus large-angular, and oblique versus large-angular. Holm's step-down procedure was applied across the combined family of six pairwise tests, comprising the three Euclidean comparisons and the three cosine comparisons, to account for multiple testing.\n",
            "   204: \n",
            "   205: Bootstrap confidence intervals were calculated for the identity-level mean differences in the pairwise comparisons using 20,000 bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Resampling was performed at the identity level, preserving the paired structure of the repeated-measures observations. The reported intervals are 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.\n",
            "   206: \n",
            "   207: An exploratory comparison between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions was additionally conducted using identity-level summaries. This comparison was interpreted cautiously because the two hemispheric categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions and observations and were associated with differences in angular geometry.\n",
            "   208: \n",
            "\n",
            "Match 3 | line 265 | terms: holm, wilcoxon\n",
