            "   287: \n",
            "   288: \n",
            "   289: ## 3.6 Summary of findings\n",
            "   290: \n",
            "   291: The results show progressively greater reference-relative embedding displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The identity-level repeated-measures analysis identified statistically significant group differences for both distance measures, and all six pairwise comparisons remained significant following combined six-test Holm correction. The exploratory hemisphere analysis also showed higher mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions. These findings characterise embedding displacement within the evaluated dataset and fixed pretrained model; they do not establish recognition accuracy, operational drift-detection performance, or generalisation across architectures.\n",
            "   292: \n",
            "   293: # 4. Discussion\n",
            "   294: \n",
            "   295: ## 4.1 Principal findings\n",
            "   296: \n",
            "\n",
            "Match 8 | line 313 | terms: holm\n",
            "   308: \n",
            "   309: ## 4.3 Identity-level consistency and statistical evidence\n",
            "   310: \n",
            "   311: The Friedman tests identified differences among the three illumination groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17). Kendall's W was 1.0 for both metrics. In this analysis, W = 1.0 indicates complete agreement in the ranking of the three group conditions across the evaluated identities. It should not be interpreted as perfect recognition performance, perfect embedding quality, or detector accuracy.\n",
            "   312: \n",
            "   313: All six pairwise identity-level comparisons remained statistically significant after Holm correction across the combined family of six comparisons. The consistency of the ordering across identities strengthens the descriptive evidence that the observed group pattern was not confined to a small subset of identities.\n",
            "   314: \n",
            "   315: However, the statistical evidence should be interpreted within the study design. The descriptive statistics are based on unequal numbers of image observations across the three illumination groups (646 near-frontal, 418 oblique, and 1,064 large-angular-deviation observations). In contrast, the repeated-measures analysis uses identity-level group means from the same 38 identities across all three groups. The inferential sample therefore consists of 38 matched identities, rather than the total number of image observations. This distinction is important when interpreting the descriptive and inferential results.\n",
            "   316: \n",
            "   317: ## 4.4 Relationship to previous illumination research\n",
            "   318: \n",
            "\n",
