            "Match 2 | line 203 | terms: holm, wilcoxon\n",
            "   198: \n",
            "   199: Statistical comparisons were conducted using identity-level summaries of the reference-relative distances. For each identity, the mean Euclidean displacement and mean cosine distance were calculated separately within the near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular illumination geometry groups. This aggregation provided three repeated measurements for each metric from each of the 38 identities.\n",
            "   200: \n",
            "   201: Differences across the three illumination geometry groups were evaluated using the Friedman test, a non-parametric repeated-measures procedure. The test was applied separately to Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Kendall's coefficient of concordance (W) was reported as the corresponding effect-size measure.\n",
            "   202: \n",
            "   203: Pairwise differences between illumination geometry groups were examined using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the paired identity-level summaries. Three pairwise comparisons were conducted for each distance metric: near-frontal versus oblique, near-frontal versus large-angular, and oblique versus large-angular. Holm's step-down procedure was applied across the combined family of six pairwise tests, comprising the three Euclidean comparisons and the three cosine comparisons, to account for multiple testing.\n",
            "   204: \n",
            "   205: Bootstrap confidence intervals were calculated for the identity-level mean differences in the pairwise comparisons using 20,000 bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Resampling was performed at the identity level, preserving the paired structure of the repeated-measures observations. The reported intervals are 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.\n",
            "   206: \n",
            "   207: An exploratory comparison between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions was additionally conducted using identity-level summaries. This comparison was interpreted cautiously because the two hemispheric categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions and observations and were associated with differences in angular geometry.\n",
            "   208: \n",
            "\n",
