            "Match 21 | Line 2342 | Terms: holm, wilcoxon\n",
            "  2338: **5. Statistical comparisons**\n",
            "  2339: \n",
            "  2340: Step 13 produced 108 paired statistical comparisons across the two evaluation metrics, three method pairs, nine source-target scenarios, and two detectors.\n",
            "  2341: \n",
            "  2342: The comparisons used 20 paired random-seed replicates, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests, and bootstrap 95% confidence intervals for paired mean differences. Holm correction was applied separately to the false-alarm-rate and detection-rate comparison families.\n",
            "  2343: \n",
            "  2344: A total of 51 comparisons were statistically significant after the specified Holm corrections. Statistical significance does not by itself establish practical importance or generalisability.\n",
            "  2345: \n",
            "  2346: ### Limitations\n",
            "\n",
            "Match 22 | Line 2344 | Terms: holm\n",
            "  2340: Step 13 produced 108 paired statistical comparisons across the two evaluation metrics, three method pairs, nine source-target scenarios, and two detectors.\n",
            "  2341: \n",
            "  2342: The comparisons used 20 paired random-seed replicates, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests, and bootstrap 95% confidence intervals for paired mean differences. Holm correction was applied separately to the false-alarm-rate and detection-rate comparison families.\n",
            "  2343: \n",
            "  2344: A total of 51 comparisons were statistically significant after the specified Holm corrections. Statistical significance does not by itself establish practical importance or generalisability.\n",
            "  2345: \n",
            "  2346: ### Limitations\n",
            "  2347: \n",
            "  2348: 1. **Synthetic data:** The experiment uses controlled multivariate Gaussian embedding distributions rather than real biometric embeddings.\n",
            "\n",
            "Match 23 | Line 2358 | Terms: holm\n",
            "  2354: 4. **Target-data requirements:** Methods B and C require target-condition calibration data. Method C also requires target-condition reference data. Their performance should not be presented as zero-shot transfer.\n",
            "  2355: \n",
            "  2356: 5. **Statistical sample size:** Statistical comparisons use 20 independent random-seed replicates. Larger-scale experiments are needed to assess robustness.\n",
            "  2357: \n",
            "  2358: 6. **Multiple comparisons:** Holm correction is applied separately to the two metric families. The resulting significance claims are conditional on this correction strategy.\n",
            "  2359: \n",
            "  2360: 7. **No real-world validation:** The experiment does not establish biometric recognition accuracy, demographic fairness, operational robustness, or deployment readiness.\n",
            "  2361: \n",
            "  2362: 8. **Detector scope:** Only Euclidean and Ledoit–Wolf-regularized Mahalanobis detectors are evaluated.\n",
            "\n",
            "FILE: /content/drive/MyDrive/Colab Notebooks/Experiment_18_Genuine_Face_Recognition_Embedding_Validation.ipynb\n",
            "SHA256: 7323c68dfb1959bca89adefed22d87f3c2c72a69140357bcb1404e55b334ad4f\n",
            "\n",
            "Match 1 | Line 2097 | Terms: wilcoxon\n",
            "  2093: ### Objective\n",
            "  2094: Determine whether embedding displacement differs significantly between low- and high-illumination conditions.\n",
            "  2095: \n",
            "  2096: ### Statistical Method\n",
            "  2097: Paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test.\n",
            "  2098: \n",
            "  2099: ### Hypotheses\n",
            "  2100: - H0: The paired displacement distributions do not differ systematically between illumination conditions.\n",
            "  2101: - H1: The paired displacement distributions differ systematically between illumination conditions.\n",
            "\n",
            "Match 2 | Line 2110 | Terms: wilcoxon\n",
            "  2106: ### Scientific Limitation\n",
            "  2107: Statistical significance does not establish practical biometric recognition degradation. The analysis is conditional on successful face detection in all three conditions.\n",
            "  2108: \n",
            "  2109: ### Status\n",
            "  2110: Successfully completed. A paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test was conducted to compare low- and high-illumination embedding displacement across 889 paired evaluation images. The test produced a statistic of 52,705 and a p-value of 4.708938 × 10^-80. The median paired displacement difference was 0.075521, and the mean difference was 0.083849. High-illumination displacement was greater for 77.50% of paired images. The null hypothesis was rejected at the 0.05 significance level. Because images are clustered within 13 identities, this image-level result must be interpreted alongside the identity-level analysis. Statistical significance does not establish biometric recognition degradation.\n",
            "  2111: # CELL 58 (code)\n",
            "  2112: import numpy as np\n",
            "  2113: from scipy.stats import wilcoxon\n",
            "  2114: \n",
            "\n",
            "Match 3 | Line 2113 | Terms: wilcoxon\n",
            "  2109: ### Status\n",
            "  2110: Successfully completed. A paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test was conducted to compare low- and high-illumination embedding displacement across 889 paired evaluation images. The test produced a statistic of 52,705 and a p-value of 4.708938 × 10^-80. The median paired displacement difference was 0.075521, and the mean difference was 0.083849. High-illumination displacement was greater for 77.50% of paired images. The null hypothesis was rejected at the 0.05 significance level. Because images are clustered within 13 identities, this image-level result must be interpreted alongside the identity-level analysis. Statistical significance does not establish biometric recognition degradation.\n",
            "  2111: # CELL 58 (code)\n",
            "  2112: import numpy as np\n",
            "  2113: from scipy.stats import wilcoxon\n",
            "  2114: \n",
            "  2115: # Paired displacement values\n",
