            "====================================================================================================\n",
            "CELL 27 | TYPE: markdown | EXECUTION COUNT: None\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "## Step 14: Results Summary and Limitations\n",
            "\n",
            "### Experiment Overview\n",
            "\n",
            "Experiment 16 evaluates cross-condition calibration transfer for Euclidean and Ledoit–Wolf-regularized Mahalanobis embedding-level drift detectors using synthetic multivariate Gaussian data.\n",
            "\n",
            "The experiment uses:\n",
            "\n",
            "- 20 independent random seeds.\n",
            "- 64-dimensional embeddings.\n",
            "- A window size of 20.\n",
            "- Three covariance conditions: isotropic, anisotropic, and correlated.\n",
            "- Nine source-target condition combinations.\n",
            "- Two drift detectors: Euclidean and Mahalanobis.\n",
            "\n",
            "The experiment investigates whether calibration thresholds obtained under one embedding distribution remain reliable when applied to another distribution.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Calibration Strategies\n",
            "\n",
            "Three calibration strategies are compared:\n",
            "\n",
            "1. **Method A — Frozen Source Calibration:** Uses the source-condition detector and its frozen source-condition threshold.\n",
            "2. **Method B — Target-Specific Threshold Recalibration:** Retains the source-condition detector but recalibrates its threshold using target-condition calibration data.\n",
            "3. **Method C — Target-Fitted Detector and Threshold:** Fits the detector using target-condition reference data and calibrates its threshold using target-condition calibration data.\n",
            "\n",
            "Method B and Method C require target-condition calibration data. Method C additionally requires target-condition reference data. Therefore, neither should be interpreted as zero-shot transfer.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Experimental Outputs\n",
            "\n",
            "The experiment generates:\n",
            "\n",
            "- Covariance matrix and reference dataset diagnostics.\n",
            "- Calibration scores and frozen source-condition thresholds.\n",
            "- Independent control-window scores and false-alarm summaries.\n",
            "- Drift evaluation scores and detection-rate summaries.\n",
            "- Target-specific recalibration thresholds.\n",
            "- Shared control and drift evaluation datasets.\n",
            "- Comparative control and drift evaluation results for all three methods.\n",
            "- Comparative calibration performance summaries.\n",
            "- Paired statistical comparisons using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests.\n",
            "- Bootstrap confidence intervals for paired mean differences.\n",
            "- Holm-adjusted p-values for multiple comparisons.\n",
            "- Integrity and numerical validation outputs.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Preliminary Findings\n",
            "\n",
            "**1. Cross-condition calibration transfer**\n",
            "\n",
            "Frozen source-condition calibration can produce substantial false-alarm inflation or suppression when the target distribution differs from the source distribution.\n",
            "\n",
            "For example, in the correlated-to-anisotropic transfer scenario using the Mahalanobis detector, Method A produced a mean false-alarm rate of approximately 99.59%, compared with 5.91% for Method B and 4.95% for Method C.\n",
            "\n",
            "**2. Target-specific threshold recalibration**\n",
            "\n",
            "Method B substantially improves false-alarm control in the evaluated cross-condition scenarios by recalibrating the threshold while retaining the source detector.\n",
            "\n",
            "This suggests that threshold recalibration can mitigate some distribution-dependent calibration failures without requiring the detector itself to be refitted.\n",
            "\n",
            "**3. Target-fitted detector comparison**\n",
            "\n",
            "Method C also generally brings false-alarm rates closer to the nominal 5% target. However, it does not consistently outperform Method B.\n",
            "\n",
            "Differences in detection rates between Methods B and C depend on the source-target condition and detector.\n",
            "\n",
            "**4. Detection performance and false-alarm trade-offs**\n",
            "\n",
            "Higher detection rates must be interpreted alongside false-alarm rates. A detector that flags nearly all control windows may also detect nearly all drift windows, but this does not demonstrate useful drift discrimination.\n",
            "\n",
            "**5. Statistical comparisons**\n",
            "\n",
            "Step 13 produced 108 paired statistical comparisons across the two evaluation metrics, three method pairs, nine source-target scenarios, and two detectors.\n",
            "\n",
            "The comparisons used 20 paired random-seed replicates, Wilcoxon signed-rank tests, and bootstrap 95% confidence intervals for paired mean differences. Holm correction was applied separately to the false-alarm-rate and detection-rate comparison families.\n",
            "\n",
            "A total of 51 comparisons were statistically significant after the specified Holm corrections. Statistical significance does not by itself establish practical importance or generalisability.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Limitations\n",
            "\n",
            "1. **Synthetic data:** The experiment uses controlled multivariate Gaussian embedding distributions rather than real biometric embeddings.\n",
            "\n",
            "2. **Controlled drift:** Drift is introduced through synthetic mean shifts. Other forms of distribution shift, including nonlinear, temporal, multimodal, and identity-dependent changes, are not evaluated.\n",
            "\n",
            "3. **Limited distribution conditions:** Only isotropic, anisotropic, and correlated covariance structures are investigated.\n",
            "\n",
            "4. **Target-data requirements:** Methods B and C require target-condition calibration data. Method C also requires target-condition reference data. Their performance should not be presented as zero-shot transfer.\n",
            "\n",
            "5. **Statistical sample size:** Statistical comparisons use 20 independent random-seed replicates. Larger-scale experiments are needed to assess robustness.\n",
            "\n",
            "6. **Multiple comparisons:** Holm correction is applied separately to the two metric families. The resulting significance claims are conditional on this correction strategy.\n",
            "\n",
            "7. **No real-world validation:** The experiment does not establish biometric recognition accuracy, demographic fairness, operational robustness, or deployment readiness.\n",
            "\n",
            "8. **Detector scope:** Only Euclidean and Ledoit–Wolf-regularized Mahalanobis detectors are evaluated.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Integrity and Reproducibility\n",
            "\n",
            "The original experiment's 15 programmed integrity checks passed. The subsequent comparative evaluation generated 1,080 control records, 1,080 drift records, and 54 aggregated comparison groups.\n",
            "\n",
            "The paired statistical analysis generated 108 comparisons, each using 20 seed pairs. Numerical and result-count validations passed.\n",
            "\n",
            "The experiment outputs are saved as CSV and NPZ files in the Experiment 16 results directory.\n",
            "\n",
            "These checks establish that the programmed outputs satisfy the tested numerical and structural conditions. They do not independently establish scientific validity, external validity, or real-world generalisability.\n",
            "\n",
            "### Overall Conclusion\n",
            "\n",
            "Under the evaluated synthetic conditions, frozen source-condition calibration can be highly sensitive to distribution changes. Target-specific threshold recalibration substantially improves false-alarm control, while target-fitted detector calibration provides an additional adaptation strategy.\n",
            "\n",
            "The results support further investigation of calibration transfer and target-aware recalibration for embedding-level drift detection. Validation using real biometric embeddings and more diverse distribution-shift scenarios remains necessary.\n",
            "\n",
            "4. EXPERIMENT 20 NOTEBOOK NAME CHECK\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "Experiment 20: Paired Identity-Level Evaluation of Within-Identity Embedding Drift Detection Under Multiple Controlled Biometric Acquisition Changes.ipynb\n",
            "\n",
