      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "====================================================================================================\n",
            "1. COMPLETE RESULTS SECTION\n",
            "====================================================================================================\n",
            "233: # 3. Results\n",
            "234: \n",
            "235: ## 3.1 Evaluation cohort and analysis coverage\n",
            "236: \n",
            "237: The source dataset contained 2,414 standard-illumination images from 38 identities. Of these, 2,166 observations belonged to 57 common illumination conditions. One reference capture was selected for each identity using condition A+000E+00. After excluding the 38 reference observations, the evaluation comprised 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 common evaluation conditions. All 38 identities were represented in the evaluation.\n",
            "238: \n",
            "239: The evaluation observations were classified into near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. These groups contained 646, 418, and 1,064 observations, respectively.\n",
            "240: \n",
            "241: \n",
            "242: ## 3.2 Overall reference-relative embedding displacement\n",
            "243: \n",
            "244: Across 2,128 evaluation observations, mean Euclidean displacement was 0.820496 (SD = 0.362940; median = 0.817311), with values ranging from 0.151681 to 1.522514. Mean cosine distance was 0.402439 (SD = 0.309758; median = 0.333999), ranging from 0.011504 to 1.159025.\n",
            "245: \n",
            "246: \n",
            "247: ## 3.3 Displacement across illumination geometry groups\n",
            "248: \n",
            "249: Both distance measures increased progressively across the illumination geometry groups. Mean Euclidean displacement increased from 0.401897 under near-frontal conditions to 0.708976 under oblique conditions and 1.118457 under large-angular deviation conditions. Mean cosine distance followed the same ordering, increasing from 0.086877 to 0.262274 and 0.649095, respectively.\n",
            "250: \n",
            "251: \n",
            "252: ### Table 1. Descriptive statistics by illumination geometry group\n",
            "253: \n",
            "254: | Illumination group | Observations | Identities | Conditions | Euclidean mean | Euclidean SD | Cosine mean | Cosine SD |\n",
            "255: |---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n",
            "256: | Near-frontal | 646 | 38 | 17 | 0.4019 | 0.1107 | 0.0869 | 0.0479 |\n",
            "257: | Oblique | 418 | 38 | 11 | 0.7090 | 0.1482 | 0.2623 | 0.1062 |\n",
            "258: | Large-angular deviation | 1064 | 38 | 28 | 1.1185 | 0.2175 | 0.6491 | 0.2388 |\n",
            "259: \n",
            "260: \n",
            "261: ## 3.4 Identity-level repeated-measures analysis\n",
            "262: \n",
            "263: Friedman repeated-measures tests identified differences among the three illumination geometry groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0). Each analysis included 38 identities measured across three groups.\n",
            "264: \n",
            "265: Pairwise identity-level comparisons used Wilcoxon signed-rank tests. Confidence intervals for mean paired differences were estimated using 20,000 identity-level bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Holm correction was applied across one combined family of six comparisons covering both distance measures. All six comparisons remained statistically significant.\n",
            "266: \n",
            "267: \n",
            "268: ### Table 2. Identity-level pairwise comparisons\n",
            "269: \n",
            "270: | Metric | Comparison | Mean paired difference | 95% bootstrap CI | Raw p | Six-test Holm p |\n",
            "271: |---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n",
            "272: | Euclidean displacement | Near-frontal vs Oblique | 0.307079 | [0.285544, 0.328413] | 7.276e-12 | 4.366e-11 |\n",
            "273: | Euclidean displacement | Near-frontal vs Large-angular deviation | 0.716560 | [0.691110, 0.741689] | 7.276e-12 | 4.366e-11 |\n",
            "274: | Euclidean displacement | Oblique vs Large-angular deviation | 0.409481 | [0.387346, 0.431761] | 7.276e-12 | 4.366e-11 |\n",
            "275: | Cosine distance | Near-frontal vs Oblique | 0.175397 | [0.159082, 0.191703] | 7.276e-12 | 4.366e-11 |\n",
            "276: | Cosine distance | Near-frontal vs Large-angular deviation | 0.562219 | [0.536391, 0.588301] | 7.276e-12 | 4.366e-11 |\n",
            "277: | Cosine distance | Oblique vs Large-angular deviation | 0.386821 | [0.364906, 0.408321] | 7.276e-12 | 4.366e-11 |\n",
            "278: \n",
            "279: \n",
            "280: ## 3.5 Exploratory frontal and beyond-frontal comparison\n",
            "281: \n",
            "282: In the exploratory hemisphere analysis, mean Euclidean displacement was 0.706220 under frontal conditions and 1.287990 under beyond-frontal conditions. The mean paired difference (beyond-frontal minus frontal) was 0.581770 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.561986, 0.601954]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11).\n",
            "283: \n",
            "284: For cosine distance, the corresponding means were 0.295816 and 0.838624. The mean paired difference was 0.542807 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.516419, 0.568516]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11).\n",
            "285: \n",
            "286: This comparison is exploratory. The frontal and beyond-frontal categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions, and hemisphere membership is related to angular illumination geometry. Therefore, the observed differences should not be interpreted as an isolated causal effect of hemisphere direction.\n",
            "287: \n",
            "288: \n",
            "289: ## 3.6 Summary of findings\n",
            "290: \n",
            "291: The results show progressively greater reference-relative embedding displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The identity-level repeated-measures analysis identified statistically significant group differences for both distance measures, and all six pairwise comparisons remained significant following combined six-test Holm correction. The exploratory hemisphere analysis also showed higher mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions. These findings characterise embedding displacement within the evaluated dataset and fixed pretrained model; they do not establish recognition accuracy, operational drift-detection performance, or generalisation across architectures.\n",
            "292: \n",
            "\n",
            "====================================================================================================\n",
            "2. ORIGINAL FROZEN FIGURES\n",
