            " accuracy_claim | matches: 8\n",
            "34 The results do not establish recognition accuracy, operational drift\n",
            "113 The study does not evaluate face-recognition accuracy, develop an illumination-invariant representation, establish operational drift-detection capability, or validate generalisation across independent recognition architectures. Its findings should be interpreted as an empirical characterisation of reference-relative embedding displacement under the specific dataset, preprocessing procedure, and model used in this investigation.\n",
            "127 The study was intentionally restricted to descriptive embedding-space analysis. It did not estimate calibrated detection thresholds, operational false-alarm rates, or recognition accuracy. It also did not evaluate an independent face-recognition architecture. Consequently, the results characterise reference-relative embedding displacement within the selected dataset, preprocessing procedure, and pretrained model; they do not establish that illumination alone caused the observed differences or that the findings generalise to other recognition systems.\n",
            "183 The resulting observation-level distances were subsequently grouped by illumination geometry for descriptive and identity-level statistical analysis. The distance calculations quantify changes in embedding representation relative to the selected reference capture. They do not, by themselves, establish a change in recognition accuracy, identity verification performance, or an operational biometric drift event.\n",
            "231 Finally, the study characterises reference-relative embedding displacement rather than biometric recognition performance or an operational drift-detection system. It does not establish verification accuracy, fairness, deployment readiness, or generalisation to other datasets and recognition architectures. These boundaries should be retained when interpreting the empirical findings.\n",
            "309 The results show progressively greater reference-relative embedding displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The identity-level repeated-measures analysis identified statistically significant group differences for both distance measures, and all six pairwise comparisons remained significant following combined six-test Holm correction. The exploratory hemisphere analysis also showed higher mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions. These findings characterise embedding displacement within the evaluated dataset and fixed pretrained model; they do not establish recognition accuracy, operational drift-detection performance, or generalisation across architectures.\n",
            "329 The Friedman tests identified differences among the three illumination groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17). Kendall's W was 1.0 for both metrics. In this analysis, W = 1.0 indicates complete agreement in the ranking of the three group conditions across the evaluated identities. It should not be interpreted as perfect recognition performance, perfect embedding quality, or detector accuracy.\n",
            "365 **No recognition-performance evaluation.** The study did not measure identification accuracy, verification accuracy, false acceptance, false rejection, or recognition performance under operational conditions. Embedding displacement cannot be substituted for these outcomes.\n",
            "\n",
            " fairness_claim | matches: 1\n",
            "231 Finally, the study characterises reference-relative embedding displacement rather than biometric recognition performance or an operational drift-detection system. It does not establish verification accuracy, fairness, deployment readiness, or generalisation to other datasets and recognition architectures. These boundaries should be retained when interpreting the empirical findings.\n",
            "\n",
            " deployment_claim | matches: 0\n",
            "\n",
            " generalisation_claim | matches: 5\n",
            "35 detection, or generalisation across architectures.\n",
            "113 The study does not evaluate face-recognition accuracy, develop an illumination-invariant representation, establish operational drift-detection capability, or validate generalisation across independent recognition architectures. Its findings should be interpreted as an empirical characterisation of reference-relative embedding displacement under the specific dataset, preprocessing procedure, and model used in this investigation.\n",
            "231 Finally, the study characterises reference-relative embedding displacement rather than biometric recognition performance or an operational drift-detection system. It does not establish verification accuracy, fairness, deployment readiness, or generalisation to other datasets and recognition architectures. These boundaries should be retained when interpreting the empirical findings.\n",
            "309 The results show progressively greater reference-relative embedding displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The identity-level repeated-measures analysis identified statistically significant group differences for both distance measures, and all six pairwise comparisons remained significant following combined six-test Holm correction. The exploratory hemisphere analysis also showed higher mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions. These findings characterise embedding displacement within the evaluated dataset and fixed pretrained model; they do not establish recognition accuracy, operational drift-detection performance, or generalisation across architectures.\n",
            "387 The findings contribute a focused empirical characterisation of embedding displacement under the specified dataset, reference-selection procedure, preprocessing pipeline, and pretrained model. They should be interpreted as descriptive evidence rather than proof of recognition failure, causal illumination effects, generalisation across architectures, or operational drift-detection capability.\n",
            "\n",
            " causal_claim | matches: 2\n",
            "217 The hemisphere comparison was treated as exploratory and secondary to the three-group illumination geometry analysis. The two categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions and observations. In addition, hemisphere membership was associated with differences in angular illumination geometry. Consequently, the comparison does not isolate a hemisphere effect independently of the underlying angular distribution and should not be interpreted as evidence of a causal effect of illumination direction.\n",
            "304 This comparison is exploratory. The frontal and beyond-frontal categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions, and hemisphere membership is related to angular illumination geometry. Therefore, the observed differences should not be interpreted as an isolated causal effect of hemisphere direction.\n",
