        "# Experiment 20 — Phase 4.5: Manuscript-Level Statistical Methodology Verification\n",
        "\n",
        "## Objective\n",
        "Inspect the frozen manuscript to determine whether its statistical methodology and interpretation adequately address the issues identified in the Phase 4.5 audit.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Review targets\n",
        "1. Inferential unit and identity-level aggregation.\n",
        "2. Friedman repeated-measures test and its assumptions.\n",
        "3. Paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test and interpretation.\n",
        "4. Holm correction across the six primary pairwise comparisons.\n",
        "5. Separate Holm correction for the two exploratory hemisphere comparisons.\n",
        "6. Identity-level bootstrap procedure and confidence intervals.\n",
        "7. Mathematical dependence between Euclidean displacement and cosine distance.\n",
        "8. Explicit limitations concerning unequal illumination-condition counts and hemisphere confounding.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Preservation\n",
        "- Read the frozen V2 manuscript only.\n",
        "- Do not edit or overwrite the manuscript.\n",
        "- Do not change statistical results or historical evidence.\n",
        "- Record the exact manuscript hash before and after inspection.\n",
        "- Distinguish between statements explicitly present and issues that remain unaddressed."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "SO6nfVMQVQOs"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# =================================================================================================\n",
        "# EXPERIMENT 20 — PHASE 4.5\n",
        "# MANUSCRIPT-LEVEL STATISTICAL METHODOLOGY VERIFICATION\n",
        "# READ-ONLY AUDIT\n",
        "# =================================================================================================\n",
        "\n",
        "import os\n",
        "import re\n",
        "import json\n",
        "import hashlib\n",
        "from datetime import datetime, timezone\n",
        "\n",
        "MANUSCRIPT_PATH = (\n",
        "    \"/content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/\"\n",
        "    \"step_3_3/step_3330/experiment20_integrated_manuscript_v2.md\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "EXPECTED_SHA256 = (\n",
        "    \"2cc4156af63987fae717c37e997e6478e4253d81f584854fb5fab9f18da5e752\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "OUTPUT_DIR = (\n",
        "    \"/content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/\"\n",
        "    \"step_4_5_statistical_methodology_audit\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "OUTPUT_PATH = (\n",
        "    OUTPUT_DIR\n",
        "    + \"/experiment20_phase45_manuscript_methodology_review.json\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "os.makedirs(OUTPUT_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "def sha256_file(path):\n",
        "    h = hashlib.sha256()\n",
        "    with open(path, \"rb\") as f:\n",
        "        for chunk in iter(lambda: f.read(1024 * 1024), b\"\"):\n",
        "            h.update(chunk)\n",
        "    return h.hexdigest()\n",
        "\n",
        "assert os.path.isfile(MANUSCRIPT_PATH), (\n",
        "    \"Frozen V2 manuscript not found at the recorded path.\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "before_hash = sha256_file(MANUSCRIPT_PATH)\n",
        "\n",
        "with open(MANUSCRIPT_PATH, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n",
        "    manuscript = f.read()\n",
        "\n",
        "lines = manuscript.splitlines()\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"=\" * 100)\n",
        "print(\"EXPERIMENT 20 — PHASE 4.5 MANUSCRIPT REVIEW\")\n",
        "print(\"=\" * 100)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\n1. MANUSCRIPT INTEGRITY\")\n",
        "print(\"Path:\", MANUSCRIPT_PATH)\n",
        "print(\"Actual SHA256:\", before_hash)\n",
        "print(\"Expected SHA256:\", EXPECTED_SHA256)\n",
        "print(\"Hash matches frozen V2:\", before_hash == EXPECTED_SHA256)\n",
        "print(\"Total lines:\", len(lines))\n",
        "print(\"Total words:\", len(manuscript.split()))\n",
        "\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "# 2. FIND RELEVANT PASSAGES\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "\n",
        "topics = {\n",
        "    \"inferential_unit\": [\n",
        "        r\"inferential unit\",\n",
        "        r\"identity.level\",\n",
        "        r\"38 identities\",\n",
        "        r\"independent observations\",\n",
        "        r\"unit of analysis\"\n",
        "    ],\n",
        "    \"friedman_test\": [\n",
        "        r\"friedman\",\n",
        "        r\"repeated.measures\",\n",
        "        r\"rank\"\n",
        "    ],\n",
        "    \"wilcoxon_test\": [\n",
        "        r\"wilcoxon\",\n",
        "        r\"signed.rank\",\n",
        "        r\"paired differences\",\n",
        "        r\"symmetr\"\n",
        "    ],\n",
        "    \"holm_correction\": [\n",
        "        r\"holm\",\n",
        "        r\"multiple comparison\",\n",
        "        r\"family\",\n",
        "        r\"adjusted p\"\n",
        "    ],\n",
        "    \"bootstrap\": [\n",
        "        r\"bootstrap\",\n",
        "        r\"resampling\",\n",
        "        r\"percentile\",\n",
        "        r\"20,000\",\n",
        "        r\"20000\"\n",
        "    ],\n",
        "    \"metric_dependence\": [\n",
        "        r\"cosine distance\",\n",
        "        r\"euclidean\",\n",
        "        r\"L2.normal\",\n",
        "        r\"normalis\",\n",
        "        r\"normaliz\",\n",
        "        r\"mathematically related\",\n",
        "        r\"monotonic\"\n",
        "    ],\n",
        "    \"hemisphere_analysis\": [\n",
        "        r\"hemisphere\",\n",
        "        r\"frontal\",\n",
        "        r\"beyond.frontal\",\n",
        "        r\"exploratory\"\n",
        "    ],\n",
        "    \"unequal_condition_counts\": [\n",
        "        r\"unequal\",\n",
        "        r\"condition counts\",\n",
        "        r\"number of conditions\",\n",
        "        r\"illumination conditions\",\n",
        "        r\"angular geometry\",\n",
        "        r\"confound\"\n",
        "    ]\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "topic_matches = {}\n",
        "\n",
        "for topic, patterns in topics.items():\n",
        "    matched_lines = []\n",
        "\n",
        "    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\n",
        "        if any(re.search(pattern, line, re.IGNORECASE) for pattern in patterns):\n",
        "            matched_lines.append({\n",
        "                \"line_number\": i + 1,\n",
        "                \"text\": line.strip()\n",
        "            })\n",
        "\n",
        "    topic_matches[topic] = matched_lines\n",
        "\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "# 3. PRINT MATCHED PASSAGES WITH CONTEXT\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\n2. RELEVANT MANUSCRIPT PASSAGES\")\n",
        "\n",
        "for topic, matches in topic_matches.items():\n",
        "    print(\"\\n\" + \"-\" * 100)\n",
        "    print(\"TOPIC:\", topic)\n",
        "    print(\"Matching lines:\", len(matches))\n",
        "\n",
        "    if not matches:\n",
        "        print(\"NO MATCHES FOUND\")\n",
        "        continue\n",
        "\n",
        "    # Merge nearby line ranges to avoid excessive duplicate output.\n",
        "    selected = set()\n",
        "\n",
        "    for match in matches:\n",
        "        center = match[\"line_number\"] - 1\n",
        "        start = max(0, center - 2)\n",
        "        end = min(len(lines), center + 3)\n",
        "\n",
        "        for j in range(start, end):\n",
        "            selected.add(j)\n",
        "\n",
        "    for j in sorted(selected):\n",
        "        print(f\"[L{j+1}] {lines[j]}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "# 4. EXTRACT STATISTICAL METHOD SECTIONS BY HEADING\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "\n",
        "heading_positions = []\n",
        "\n",
        "for i, line in enumerate(lines):\n",
        "    if re.match(r\"^\\s{0,3}#{1,6}\\s+\", line):\n",
        "        heading_positions.append({\n",
        "            \"line_number\": i + 1,\n",
        "            \"heading\": line.strip()\n",
        "        })\n",
        "\n",
        "statistical_headings = [\n",
        "    h for h in heading_positions\n",
        "    if re.search(\n",
        "        r\"statistic|method|analysis|result|discussion|limitation|\"\n",
        "        r\"bootstrap|comparison|inferential|hemisphere\",\n",
        "        h[\"heading\"],\n",
        "        re.IGNORECASE\n",
        "    )\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 100)\n",
        "print(\"3. RELEVANT SECTION HEADINGS\")\n",
        "print(\"=\" * 100)\n",
        "\n",
        "for h in statistical_headings:\n",
        "    print(f\"[L{h['line_number']}] {h['heading']}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "# 5. PRELIMINARY PRESENCE CHECKS\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "\n",
        "presence_checks = {\n",
        "    \"inferential_unit_discussed\": len(topic_matches[\"inferential_unit\"]) > 0,\n",
        "    \"friedman_test_discussed\": len(topic_matches[\"friedman_test\"]) > 0,\n",
        "    \"wilcoxon_test_discussed\": len(topic_matches[\"wilcoxon_test\"]) > 0,\n",
        "    \"holm_correction_discussed\": len(topic_matches[\"holm_correction\"]) > 0,\n",
        "    \"bootstrap_discussed\": len(topic_matches[\"bootstrap\"]) > 0,\n",
        "    \"metric_dependence_discussed\": len(topic_matches[\"metric_dependence\"]) > 0,\n",
        "    \"hemisphere_analysis_discussed\": len(topic_matches[\"hemisphere_analysis\"]) > 0,\n",
        "    \"unequal_condition_counts_discussed\": len(topic_matches[\"unequal_condition_counts\"]) > 0\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 100)\n",
        "print(\"4. PRELIMINARY PRESENCE CHECKS\")\n",
        "print(\"=\" * 100)\n",
        "\n",
        "for name, value in presence_checks.items():\n",
        "    print((\"PRESENT\" if value else \"NOT FOUND\") + \":\", name)\n",
        "\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "# 6. SAVE REVIEW EVIDENCE\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "\n",
        "after_hash = sha256_file(MANUSCRIPT_PATH)\n",
        "\n",
        "report = {\n",
        "    \"experiment\": \"Experiment 20\",\n",
        "    \"phase\": \"4.5\",\n",
        "    \"audit_component\": \"Manuscript-Level Statistical Methodology Verification\",\n",
        "    \"created_utc\": datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(),\n",
        "    \"manuscript_path\": MANUSCRIPT_PATH,\n",
        "    \"manuscript_sha256_before\": before_hash,\n",
        "    \"manuscript_sha256_after\": after_hash,\n",
        "    \"expected_frozen_sha256\": EXPECTED_SHA256,\n",
        "    \"frozen_hash_valid\": before_hash == EXPECTED_SHA256,\n",
        "    \"manuscript_unchanged\": before_hash == after_hash,\n",
        "    \"total_lines\": len(lines),\n",
        "    \"total_words\": len(manuscript.split()),\n",
        "    \"relevant_headings\": statistical_headings,\n",
        "    \"topic_matches\": topic_matches,\n",
        "    \"preliminary_presence_checks\": presence_checks,\n",
        "    \"interpretation\": (\n",
        "        \"Keyword presence is not proof that an assumption or limitation is \"\n",
        "        \"adequately explained. The matched passages require substantive review.\"\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    \"manuscript_modified\": False\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "with open(OUTPUT_PATH, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n",
        "    json.dump(report, f, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)\n",
        "\n",
        "with open(OUTPUT_PATH, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n",
        "    verified = json.load(f)\n",
        "\n",
        "report_hash = sha256_file(OUTPUT_PATH)\n",
        "\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "# 7. FINAL STATUS\n",
        "# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 100)\n",
        "print(\"5. FINAL PRESERVATION CHECK\")\n",
        "print(\"=\" * 100)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Manuscript hash before:\", before_hash)\n",
        "print(\"Manuscript hash after:\", after_hash)\n",
        "print(\"Manuscript unchanged:\", before_hash == after_hash)\n",
        "print(\"Frozen V2 hash valid:\", before_hash == EXPECTED_SHA256)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\nReview evidence saved:\")\n",
        "print(OUTPUT_PATH)\n",
        "print(\"Report SHA256:\", report_hash)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\nIMPORTANT:\")\n",
        "print(\"This is a passage-discovery audit, not an automatic methodological approval.\")\n",
        "print(\"Do not edit the manuscript based only on keyword matches.\")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"\\n\" + \"=\" * 100)\n",
        "print(\"PHASE 4.5 MANUSCRIPT REVIEW EXTRACTION COMPLETED\")\n",
        "print(\"=\" * 100)"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "9xgkjez8VZ4T",
        "outputId": "07208f84-9379-43ba-a637-34c0de9cd673"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "====================================================================================================\n",
            "EXPERIMENT 20 — PHASE 4.5 MANUSCRIPT REVIEW\n",
            "====================================================================================================\n",
            "\n",
            "1. MANUSCRIPT INTEGRITY\n",
            "Path: /content/drive/MyDrive/Experiment_20_Results/step_3_3/step_3330/experiment20_integrated_manuscript_v2.md\n",
            "Actual SHA256: 2cc4156af63987fae717c37e997e6478e4253d81f584854fb5fab9f18da5e752\n",
            "Expected SHA256: 2cc4156af63987fae717c37e997e6478e4253d81f584854fb5fab9f18da5e752\n",
            "Hash matches frozen V2: True\n",
            "Total lines: 396\n",
            "Total words: 6925\n",
            "\n",
            "2. RELEVANT MANUSCRIPT PASSAGES\n",
            "\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "TOPIC: inferential_unit\n",
            "Matching lines: 46\n",
            "[L1] # Reference-Relative Face Embedding Displacement Across Illumination Geometry Conditions: An Identity-Level Empirical Study\n",
            "[L2] \n",
            "[L3] ## Abstract\n",
            "[L9] geometry conditions using a fixed pretrained FaceNet architecture.\n",
            "[L10] Experiments were conducted on the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset,\n",
            "[L11] comprising 38 identities and 2,414 images. A common reference capture\n",
            "[L12] was established for each identity, and 2,128 evaluation observations\n",
            "[L13] across 56 non-reference illumination conditions were analysed using\n",
            "[L69] Existing research has examined the effects of illumination variation on face recognition through several approaches, including illumination preprocessing, feature representation, subject-specific illumination spaces, and recognition-performance evaluation. Studies using Yale B and Extended Yale B datasets have also investigated changes in facial representations under different lighting directions. More recent work has explored deep face-recognition architectures and the geometric properties of learned embedding spaces.\n",
            "[L70] \n",
            "[L71] Despite this established body of research, there remains a need for focused empirical characterisation of how reference-relative embedding displacement varies across illumination geometry conditions at the individual-identity level within a fixed pretrained face-recognition model. In particular, comparing multiple distance measures relative to a common reference capture provides a structured way to examine whether displacement patterns differ across illumination groups and whether these differences are consistent across identities.\n",
            "[L72] \n",
            "[L73] This study addresses that specific empirical question using a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model and the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset. Rather than developing an illumination-invariant representation or evaluating identity-classification performance, the investigation examines Euclidean displacement and cosine distance between identity-specific reference embeddings and embeddings from other captures of the same individuals.\n",
            "[L74] \n",
            "[L75] The motivation is to provide a transparent, identity-level description of embedding-space variation across defined illumination geometry conditions. By examining both observation-level distributions and repeated-measures differences across identities, the study seeks to characterise the consistency and magnitude of reference-relative displacement within the evaluated dataset.\n",
            "[L76] \n",
            "[L77] The resulting analysis is deliberately descriptive. Because the evaluation uses different image captures rather than exact transformations of identical source images, observed embedding differences cannot be attributed exclusively to illumination. The findings are also specific to the selected dataset, preprocessing procedure, and pretrained model. Accordingly, the study is intended to contribute a focused empirical characterisation rather than a general claim about all face-recognition systems.\n",
            "[L92] 2. To quantify reference-relative embedding displacement using Euclidean distance and cosine distance.\n",
            "[L93] 3. To classify common illumination conditions into near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular geometry groups.\n",
            "[L94] 4. To compare displacement measurements across illumination groups using identity-level repeated-measures statistical analysis.\n",
            "[L95] 5. To explore differences between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions while accounting for the unequal number of evaluated conditions.\n",
            "[L96] \n",
            "[L101] This study makes four empirical contributions.\n",
            "[L102] \n",
            "[L103] First, it provides an identity-level characterisation of reference-relative face embedding displacement across three illumination geometry groups: near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular conditions. The analysis examines how the magnitude of displacement varies across individual identities within the evaluated dataset.\n",
            "[L104] \n",
            "[L105] Second, it compares two complementary embedding-space distance measures: Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Using both measures provides a broader descriptive examination of changes in embedding representations relative to an identity-specific reference capture.\n",
            "[L109] Fourth, it provides an exploratory comparison of frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions, examining whether reference-relative displacement differs between these broader geometric categories.\n",
            "[L110] \n",
            "[L111] The scope of the study is deliberately limited. The evaluation uses the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset, comprising 38 identities and 2,414 standard-illumination images, and a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model. Of the 2,414 standard-illumination images, 2,166 observations belong to the 57 common illumination conditions. After excluding the reference condition, 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 evaluation conditions are included in the displacement analysis. Each identity is represented by one reference capture, against which embeddings from other captures are compared. The evaluation therefore examines different identity-matched captures rather than exact transformations of identical source images.\n",
            "[L112] \n",
            "[L113] The study does not evaluate face-recognition accuracy, develop an illumination-invariant representation, establish operational drift-detection capability, or validate generalisation across independent recognition architectures. Its findings should be interpreted as an empirical characterisation of reference-relative embedding displacement under the specific dataset, preprocessing procedure, and model used in this investigation.\n",
            "[L119] Experiment 20 employed a descriptive empirical design to examine reference-relative displacement in deep face-recognition embeddings across illumination geometry conditions. The analysis focused on the extent to which the embedding representation of an identity's facial image differed from that identity's embedding under a designated reference illumination condition. Rather than developing or evaluating an operational drift-detection algorithm, the experiment characterised these differences using two distance measures: Euclidean displacement and cosine distance.\n",
            "[L120] \n",
            "[L121] The evaluation used the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset, comprising 2,414 standard-illumination images from 38 identities. A common-condition selection procedure identified 57 illumination conditions represented across all 38 identities, yielding 2,166 identity-condition observations. One observation per identity corresponded to the designated reference condition, A+000E+00. These 38 reference observations were used to establish the identity-specific reference embeddings. After excluding the reference condition, the primary evaluation comprised 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 illumination conditions.\n",
            "[L122] \n",
            "[L123] The primary unit of observation was an identity-condition pair, while identity served as the repeated-measures unit for group-level statistical comparisons. This structure allowed displacement measurements from the same 38 identities to be examined across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The observations represent identity-condition-matched captures under different illumination conditions; they are not pairs produced by applying controlled transformations to identical source images.\n",
            "[L124] \n",
            "[L125] The analysis was conducted using 512-dimensional embeddings generated by a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model with VGGFace2 weights. Each evaluation embedding was compared with the corresponding identity's reference embedding. Euclidean displacement and cosine distance were then summarised across illumination geometry groups and examined using identity-level repeated-measures statistical procedures.\n",
            "[L126] \n",
            "[L127] The study was intentionally restricted to descriptive embedding-space analysis. It did not estimate calibrated detection thresholds, operational false-alarm rates, or recognition accuracy. It also did not evaluate an independent face-recognition architecture. Consequently, the results characterise reference-relative embedding displacement within the selected dataset, preprocessing procedure, and pretrained model; they do not establish that illumination alone caused the observed differences or that the findings generalise to other recognition systems.\n",
            "[L129] ## 2.2 Dataset and Acquisition Provenance\n",
            "[L130] \n",
            "[L131] The external evaluation dataset used in Experiment 20 was the Cropped Extended Yale B frontal-pose illumination subset. It contains cropped grayscale facial images captured under different illumination conditions for 38 identities. This subset should be distinguished from the complete multi-pose Extended Yale B dataset.\n",
            "[L132] \n",
            "[L133] The dataset archive was acquired through a community-hosted Academic Torrents listing. The acquisition record identifies the source listing and records an archive size of 58,493,820 bytes. The downloaded ZIP archive passed its integrity test and contained 2,586 members. Its SHA-256 checksum was recorded as 8bbabf3836d077d60626c9ee3c54c2d2851a8d2c15c8b7e13b9bc162e86ea573. The archive provenance remains subject to an important limitation: it was not independently authenticated against an official UCSD checksum. Accordingly, the acquisition record and checksum provide traceability for the downloaded archive but do not establish official dataset authenticity.\n",
            "[L134] \n",
            "[L135] The archive contained 2,452 PGM image files, all of which were successfully decoded during the image-integrity audit. Filename and condition validation identified 2,414 standard-illumination images and 38 ambient reference images. The ambient images were excluded from the standard-illumination analysis. The retained standard-illumination images represented 64 distinct illumination conditions across the 38 identities. Seven identities did not contain every condition in the overall set.\n",
            "[L136] \n",
            "[L137] To support comparisons with complete identity coverage, the analysis used the 57 illumination conditions shared by all 38 identities. This common-condition subset comprised 2,166 identity-condition observations. The remaining conditions that were not shared by every identity were not included in analyses requiring complete identity-condition coverage. One observation per identity corresponded to the reference condition, A+000E+00; after its exclusion, 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 conditions formed the primary evaluation cohort.\n",
            "[L138] \n",
            "[L139] The dataset was frozen for the Stage 2 analysis, with acquisition records, image-integrity results, condition manifests, embedding artifacts, and associated checksums retained in the project archive. These records support reproducibility and traceability of the analysed cohort. They do not remove the limitation arising from the community-hosted source or establish authentication against an official dataset checksum.\n",
            "[L141] ## 2.3 Subject Selection and Illumination-Condition Coverage\n",
            "[L142] \n",
            "[L143] The subject cohort comprised 38 identities represented in the Cropped Extended Yale B frontal-pose illumination subset. The standard-illumination image collection contained 64 distinct illumination conditions across the cohort; however, not every condition was available for every identity. Seven identities had incomplete condition coverage. To preserve a consistent identity set across the primary condition-based comparisons, the analysis retained only conditions represented for all 38 identities.\n",
            "[L144] \n",
            "[L145] This complete-case condition selection produced 57 common illumination conditions and 2,166 identity-condition observations, corresponding to 38 observations for each condition. The selection was based on shared condition availability rather than on embedding displacement values or statistical outcomes. Conditions not represented across all 38 identities were excluded from analyses requiring complete identity-condition coverage.\n",
            "[L146] \n",
            "[L147] The condition A+000E+00 was designated as the reference condition. One reference observation was available for each of the 38 identities, providing an identity-specific reference embedding. The reference observations were retained for calculating reference-relative distances but excluded from the non-reference evaluation set. Consequently, the primary evaluation comprised the remaining 56 common conditions and 2,128 identity-condition observations.\n",
            "[L148] \n",
            "[L149] The 56 non-reference conditions were assigned to three illumination geometry groups using the frozen condition-grouping manifest: near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation. The corresponding evaluation counts were 17, 11, and 28 conditions, respectively, representing 646, 418, and 1,064 observations. The reference condition belonged to the near-frontal group but was excluded from these evaluation counts.\n",
            "[L150] \n",
            "[L151] The geometry groups represent the angular deviation of the illumination direction relative to the camera axis, as defined by the study's condition-grouping procedure. They do not represent directly measured illumination intensity. The same 38 identities were represented in each of the three evaluation groups, enabling identity-level repeated-measures comparisons. The resulting observations are identity-condition-matched captures under different lighting conditions, not exact image-level pairs generated from a single source image.\n",
            "[L152] \n",
            "[L153] ## 2.4 Face-Image Preprocessing\n",
            "[L175] ## 2.6 Embedding Normalisation and Reference-Relative Distance Calculation\n",
            "[L176] \n",
            "[L177] The extracted 512-dimensional embeddings were L2-normalised before the reference-relative displacement analysis. For each of the 38 identities, the embedding associated with the reference illumination condition A+000E+00 served as that identity's reference representation. This produced 38 identity-specific reference embeddings.\n",
            "[L178] \n",
            "[L179] Each non-reference embedding was matched to the reference embedding belonging to the same identity. The evaluation therefore compared identity-condition observations with their corresponding within-identity reference representations rather than using a single reference vector shared across all subjects. After excluding the reference observations, the analysis included 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 common evaluation conditions.\n",
            "[L181] Two distance measures were used to characterise reference-relative embedding displacement. Euclidean displacement measured the L2 distance between an evaluation embedding and its corresponding identity-specific reference embedding. Cosine distance was calculated as one minus the cosine similarity between the same pair of embedding vectors. Both measures were calculated for each evaluation observation and retained alongside its identity and illumination-condition metadata. Because the embeddings were L2-normalised, the two distances are mathematically related: squared Euclidean distance equals twice cosine distance. They therefore provide related, rather than independent, measures of embedding displacement.\n",
            "[L182] \n",
            "[L183] The resulting observation-level distances were subsequently grouped by illumination geometry for descriptive and identity-level statistical analysis. The distance calculations quantify changes in embedding representation relative to the selected reference capture. They do not, by themselves, establish a change in recognition accuracy, identity verification performance, or an operational biometric drift event.\n",
            "[L184] \n",
            "[L185] The analysis was descriptive: no operational detection thresholds, calibrated false-alarm rates, or classification decisions were derived from these distances. The reference-relative comparisons should also be interpreted in light of the use of different image captures across illumination conditions, rather than exact transformations of identical source images.\n",
            "[L191] Three study-specific illumination geometry groups were defined. Near-frontal conditions comprised observations with θ ≤ 30°. Oblique conditions comprised observations with 30° < θ ≤ 60°. Large-angular conditions comprised observations with θ > 60°. These mutually exclusive intervals assigned each evaluated illumination condition to one geometry group.\n",
            "[L192] \n",
            "[L193] Across the 56 non-reference evaluation conditions, 17 conditions were classified as near-frontal, 11 as oblique, and 28 as large-angular. Across the 38 identities, these groups contained 646, 418, and 1,064 evaluation observations, respectively. All three groups therefore included observations from the same 38 identities, supporting the repeated-measures comparisons conducted at the identity level.\n",
            "[L194] \n",
            "[L195] The groups represent the angular geometry of the illumination direction rather than its intensity. They should not be interpreted as brightness levels, exposure categories, or measurements of lighting power. The three angular boundaries were selected for this study and are not presented as universal or standard illumination categories. Previous face-recognition research has used angular subsets to examine illumination variation, but the thresholds and grouping scheme used here are study-specific.\n",
            "[L197] ## 2.8 Statistical Analysis\n",
            "[L198] \n",
            "[L199] Statistical comparisons were conducted using identity-level summaries of the reference-relative distances. For each identity, the mean Euclidean displacement and mean cosine distance were calculated separately within the near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular illumination geometry groups. This aggregation provided three repeated measurements for each metric from each of the 38 identities.\n",
            "[L200] \n",
            "[L201] Differences across the three illumination geometry groups were evaluated using the Friedman test, a non-parametric repeated-measures procedure. The test was applied separately to Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Kendall's coefficient of concordance (W) was reported as the corresponding effect-size measure.\n",
            "[L202] \n",
            "[L203] Pairwise differences between illumination geometry groups were examined using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the paired identity-level summaries. Three pairwise comparisons were conducted for each distance metric: near-frontal versus oblique, near-frontal versus large-angular, and oblique versus large-angular. Holm's step-down procedure was applied across the combined family of six pairwise tests, comprising the three Euclidean comparisons and the three cosine comparisons, to account for multiple testing.\n",
            "[L204] \n",
            "[L205] Bootstrap confidence intervals were calculated for the identity-level mean differences in the pairwise comparisons using 20,000 bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Resampling was performed at the identity level, preserving the paired structure of the repeated-measures observations. The reported intervals are 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.\n",
            "[L206] \n",
            "[L207] An exploratory comparison between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions was additionally conducted using identity-level summaries. This comparison was interpreted cautiously because the two hemispheric categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions and observations and were associated with differences in angular geometry.\n",
            "[L208] \n",
            "[L209] The research hypotheses were formulated retrospectively after the experimental results had been examined. They were not preregistered and should therefore be interpreted as hypotheses used to organise the analysis rather than as prospectively specified tests. Statistical significance was assessed using the corrected pairwise p-values, while effect sizes and confidence intervals were considered alongside the test results.\n",
            "[L211] ## 2.9 Exploratory Hemisphere-Based Analysis\n",
            "[L212] \n",
            "[L213] A secondary exploratory analysis compared reference-relative embedding displacement between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions. The grouping was based on the angular hemisphere classification recorded in the Stage 2 analysis. The frontal category comprised 45 non-reference evaluation conditions, corresponding to 1,710 observations across the 38 identities. The beyond-frontal category comprised 11 evaluation conditions and 418 observations.\n",
            "[L214] \n",
            "[L215] For each identity, mean Euclidean displacement and mean cosine distance were calculated separately for the frontal and beyond-frontal categories. The resulting identity-level summaries were compared using paired statistical tests, with the identity serving as the unit of comparison. This approach retained the within-identity structure of the analysis.\n",
            "[L216] \n",
            "[L217] The hemisphere comparison was treated as exploratory and secondary to the three-group illumination geometry analysis. The two categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions and observations. In addition, hemisphere membership was associated with differences in angular illumination geometry. Consequently, the comparison does not isolate a hemisphere effect independently of the underlying angular distribution and should not be interpreted as evidence of a causal effect of illumination direction.\n",
            "[L235] ## 3.1 Evaluation cohort and analysis coverage\n",
            "[L236] \n",
            "[L237] The source dataset contained 2,414 standard-illumination images from 38 identities. Of these, 2,166 observations belonged to 57 common illumination conditions. One reference capture was selected for each identity using condition A+000E+00. After excluding the 38 reference observations, the evaluation comprised 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 common evaluation conditions. All 38 identities were represented in the evaluation.\n",
            "[L238] \n",
            "[L239] The evaluation observations were classified into near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. These groups contained 646, 418, and 1,064 observations, respectively.\n",
            "[L247] ## 3.3 Displacement across illumination geometry groups\n",
            "[L248] \n",
            "[L249] Both distance measures increased progressively across the illumination geometry groups. Mean Euclidean displacement increased from 0.401897 under near-frontal conditions to 0.708976 under oblique conditions and 1.118457 under large-angular deviation conditions. Mean cosine distance followed the same ordering, increasing from 0.086877 to 0.262274 and 0.649095, respectively. The identity-level patterns are shown in Figures 1 and 2.\n",
            "[L250] \n",
            "[L251] \n",
            "[L259] \n",
            "[L260] \n",
            "[L261] ![Identity-level Euclidean displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups.](figures/experiment20_figure1_euclidean_illumination_groups.png)\n",
            "[L262] \n",
            "[L263] *Figure 1. Identity-level Euclidean displacement across illumination geometry groups. Thin lines represent the mean displacement for each of the 38 identities within each group; the thick olive line represents the mean across identities.*\n",
            "[L264] \n",
            "[L265] ![Identity-level cosine distance across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups.](figures/experiment20_figure2_cosine_illumination_groups.png)\n",
            "[L266] \n",
            "[L267] *Figure 2. Identity-level cosine distance across illumination geometry groups. Thin lines represent the mean distance for each of the 38 identities within each group; the thick olive line represents the mean across identities.*\n",
            "[L268] \n",
            "[L269] \n",
            "[L270] ## 3.4 Identity-level repeated-measures analysis\n",
            "[L271] \n",
            "[L272] Friedman repeated-measures tests identified differences among the three illumination geometry groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0). Each analysis included 38 identities measured across three groups.\n",
            "[L273] \n",
            "[L274] Pairwise identity-level comparisons used Wilcoxon signed-rank tests. Confidence intervals for mean paired differences were estimated using 20,000 identity-level bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Holm correction was applied across one combined family of six comparisons covering both distance measures. All six comparisons remained statistically significant.\n",
            "[L275] \n",
            "[L276] \n",
            "[L277] ### Table 2. Identity-level pairwise comparisons\n",
            "[L278] \n",
            "[L279] | Metric | Comparison | Mean paired difference | 95% bootstrap CI | Raw p | Six-test Holm p |\n",
            "[L293] For cosine distance, the corresponding means were 0.295816 and 0.838624. The mean paired difference was 0.542807 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.516419, 0.568516]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11). (Figure 4).\n",
            "[L294] \n",
            "[L295] ![Paired identity-level Euclidean displacement for frontal and beyond-frontal illumination hemispheres.](figures/experiment20_figure3_euclidean_hemisphere_comparison.png)\n",
            "[L296] \n",
            "[L297] *Figure 3. Paired identity-level Euclidean displacement by illumination hemisphere. Each thin line connects the frontal and beyond-frontal mean displacement for one identity; the thick olive line represents the mean across the 38 identities. This comparison is exploratory.*\n",
            "[L298] \n",
            "[L299] ![Paired identity-level cosine distance for frontal and beyond-frontal illumination hemispheres.](figures/experiment20_figure4_cosine_hemisphere_comparison.png)\n",
            "[L300] \n",
            "[L301] *Figure 4. Paired identity-level cosine distance by illumination hemisphere. Each thin line connects the frontal and beyond-frontal mean distance for one identity; the thick olive line represents the mean across the 38 identities. This comparison is exploratory.*\n",
            "[L302] \n",
            "[L303] \n",
            "[L307] ## 3.6 Summary of findings\n",
            "[L308] \n",
            "[L309] The results show progressively greater reference-relative embedding displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The identity-level repeated-measures analysis identified statistically significant group differences for both distance measures, and all six pairwise comparisons remained significant following combined six-test Holm correction. The exploratory hemisphere analysis also showed higher mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions. These findings characterise embedding displacement within the evaluated dataset and fixed pretrained model; they do not establish recognition accuracy, operational drift-detection performance, or generalisation across architectures.\n",
            "[L310] \n",
            "[L311] # 4. Discussion\n",
            "[L325] The analysis also compares different captures of each identity. Changes between captures may include image variation beyond illumination alone. Therefore, the results do not isolate a causal illumination effect. A controlled experiment using relit versions of the same source images would be required to isolate that effect more directly.\n",
            "[L326] \n",
            "[L327] ## 4.3 Identity-level consistency and statistical evidence\n",
            "[L328] \n",
            "[L329] The Friedman tests identified differences among the three illumination groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17). Kendall's W was 1.0 for both metrics. In this analysis, W = 1.0 indicates complete agreement in the ranking of the three group conditions across the evaluated identities. It should not be interpreted as perfect recognition performance, perfect embedding quality, or detector accuracy.\n",
            "[L330] \n",
            "[L331] All six pairwise identity-level comparisons remained statistically significant after Holm correction across the combined family of six comparisons. The consistency of the ordering across identities strengthens the descriptive evidence that the observed group pattern was not confined to a small subset of identities.\n",
            "[L332] \n",
            "[L333] However, the statistical evidence should be interpreted within the study design. The descriptive statistics are based on unequal numbers of image observations across the three illumination groups (646 near-frontal, 418 oblique, and 1,064 large-angular-deviation observations). In contrast, the repeated-measures analysis uses identity-level group means from the same 38 identities across all three groups. The inferential sample therefore consists of 38 matched identities, rather than the total number of image observations. This distinction is important when interpreting the descriptive and inferential results.\n",
            "[L334] \n",
            "[L335] ## 4.4 Relationship to previous illumination research\n",
            "[L377] A second direction is to evaluate additional face-recognition architectures using a consistent cohort and clearly documented preprocessing. This would determine whether the observed geometry-related displacement pattern is specific to the evaluated FaceNet model or is also present in other learned representations.\n",
            "[L378] \n",
            "[L379] A further extension would connect embedding displacement to recognition outcomes. Genuine and impostor comparisons, verification metrics, and identity-level performance analysis would be necessary to determine whether larger displacement corresponds to a measurable reduction in recognition performance.\n",
            "[L380] \n",
            "[L381] Finally, a separate drift-detection study could investigate calibrated thresholds, temporal monitoring, false-alarm behaviour, and detection delay under controlled changes in the input distribution. Such work would require its own experimental protocol and validation; it is not demonstrated by the present descriptive analysis.\n",
            "[L383] ## 4.8 Overall discussion\n",
            "[L384] \n",
            "[L385] Experiment 20 provides an identity-level descriptive analysis of reference-relative FaceNet embedding displacement across illumination geometry conditions in the evaluated Cropped Extended Yale B cohort. The results show an ordered increase in both distance measures from near-frontal to large-angular-deviation conditions, with consistent group rankings across the 38 evaluated identities. The exploratory hemisphere comparison showed a similar increase under beyond-frontal conditions, although its interpretation is limited by unequal condition coverage and its relationship to angular geometry.\n",
            "[L386] \n",
            "[L387] The findings contribute a focused empirical characterisation of embedding displacement under the specified dataset, reference-selection procedure, preprocessing pipeline, and pretrained model. They should be interpreted as descriptive evidence rather than proof of recognition failure, causal illumination effects, generalisation across architectures, or operational drift-detection capability.\n",
            "\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "TOPIC: friedman_test\n",
            "Matching lines: 19\n",
            "[L21] increased from 0.402 in near-frontal conditions to 0.709 in oblique\n",
            "[L22] conditions and 1.118 in large-angular conditions. Corresponding mean\n",
            "[L23] cosine distances increased from 0.087 to 0.262 and 0.649. Friedman\n",
            "[L24] tests indicated significant differences across groups for both\n",
            "[L25] metrics (chi-square = 76.0, p = 3.14e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0).\n",
            "[L73] This study addresses that specific empirical question using a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model and the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset. Rather than developing an illumination-invariant representation or evaluating identity-classification performance, the investigation examines Euclidean displacement and cosine distance between identity-specific reference embeddings and embeddings from other captures of the same individuals.\n",
            "[L74] \n",
            "[L75] The motivation is to provide a transparent, identity-level description of embedding-space variation across defined illumination geometry conditions. By examining both observation-level distributions and repeated-measures differences across identities, the study seeks to characterise the consistency and magnitude of reference-relative displacement within the evaluated dataset.\n",
            "[L76] \n",
            "[L77] The resulting analysis is deliberately descriptive. Because the evaluation uses different image captures rather than exact transformations of identical source images, observed embedding differences cannot be attributed exclusively to illumination. The findings are also specific to the selected dataset, preprocessing procedure, and pretrained model. Accordingly, the study is intended to contribute a focused empirical characterisation rather than a general claim about all face-recognition systems.\n",
            "[L92] 2. To quantify reference-relative embedding displacement using Euclidean distance and cosine distance.\n",
            "[L93] 3. To classify common illumination conditions into near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular geometry groups.\n",
            "[L94] 4. To compare displacement measurements across illumination groups using identity-level repeated-measures statistical analysis.\n",
            "[L95] 5. To explore differences between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions while accounting for the unequal number of evaluated conditions.\n",
            "[L96] \n",
            "[L105] Second, it compares two complementary embedding-space distance measures: Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Using both measures provides a broader descriptive examination of changes in embedding representations relative to an identity-specific reference capture.\n",
            "[L106] \n",
            "[L107] Third, it applies repeated-measures statistical analysis to examine differences between the three illumination geometry groups across the evaluated identities. This analysis considers both the magnitude of group differences and the consistency of their ordering across identities.\n",
            "[L108] \n",
            "[L109] Fourth, it provides an exploratory comparison of frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions, examining whether reference-relative displacement differs between these broader geometric categories.\n",
            "[L121] The evaluation used the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset, comprising 2,414 standard-illumination images from 38 identities. A common-condition selection procedure identified 57 illumination conditions represented across all 38 identities, yielding 2,166 identity-condition observations. One observation per identity corresponded to the designated reference condition, A+000E+00. These 38 reference observations were used to establish the identity-specific reference embeddings. After excluding the reference condition, the primary evaluation comprised 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 illumination conditions.\n",
            "[L122] \n",
            "[L123] The primary unit of observation was an identity-condition pair, while identity served as the repeated-measures unit for group-level statistical comparisons. This structure allowed displacement measurements from the same 38 identities to be examined across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The observations represent identity-condition-matched captures under different illumination conditions; they are not pairs produced by applying controlled transformations to identical source images.\n",
            "[L124] \n",
            "[L125] The analysis was conducted using 512-dimensional embeddings generated by a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model with VGGFace2 weights. Each evaluation embedding was compared with the corresponding identity's reference embedding. Euclidean displacement and cosine distance were then summarised across illumination geometry groups and examined using identity-level repeated-measures statistical procedures.\n",
            "[L126] \n",
            "[L127] The study was intentionally restricted to descriptive embedding-space analysis. It did not estimate calibrated detection thresholds, operational false-alarm rates, or recognition accuracy. It also did not evaluate an independent face-recognition architecture. Consequently, the results characterise reference-relative embedding displacement within the selected dataset, preprocessing procedure, and pretrained model; they do not establish that illumination alone caused the observed differences or that the findings generalise to other recognition systems.\n",
            "[L149] The 56 non-reference conditions were assigned to three illumination geometry groups using the frozen condition-grouping manifest: near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation. The corresponding evaluation counts were 17, 11, and 28 conditions, respectively, representing 646, 418, and 1,064 observations. The reference condition belonged to the near-frontal group but was excluded from these evaluation counts.\n",
            "[L150] \n",
            "[L151] The geometry groups represent the angular deviation of the illumination direction relative to the camera axis, as defined by the study's condition-grouping procedure. They do not represent directly measured illumination intensity. The same 38 identities were represented in each of the three evaluation groups, enabling identity-level repeated-measures comparisons. The resulting observations are identity-condition-matched captures under different lighting conditions, not exact image-level pairs generated from a single source image.\n",
            "[L152] \n",
            "[L153] ## 2.4 Face-Image Preprocessing\n",
            "[L191] Three study-specific illumination geometry groups were defined. Near-frontal conditions comprised observations with θ ≤ 30°. Oblique conditions comprised observations with 30° < θ ≤ 60°. Large-angular conditions comprised observations with θ > 60°. These mutually exclusive intervals assigned each evaluated illumination condition to one geometry group.\n",
            "[L192] \n",
            "[L193] Across the 56 non-reference evaluation conditions, 17 conditions were classified as near-frontal, 11 as oblique, and 28 as large-angular. Across the 38 identities, these groups contained 646, 418, and 1,064 evaluation observations, respectively. All three groups therefore included observations from the same 38 identities, supporting the repeated-measures comparisons conducted at the identity level.\n",
            "[L194] \n",
            "[L195] The groups represent the angular geometry of the illumination direction rather than its intensity. They should not be interpreted as brightness levels, exposure categories, or measurements of lighting power. The three angular boundaries were selected for this study and are not presented as universal or standard illumination categories. Previous face-recognition research has used angular subsets to examine illumination variation, but the thresholds and grouping scheme used here are study-specific.\n",
            "[L199] Statistical comparisons were conducted using identity-level summaries of the reference-relative distances. For each identity, the mean Euclidean displacement and mean cosine distance were calculated separately within the near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular illumination geometry groups. This aggregation provided three repeated measurements for each metric from each of the 38 identities.\n",
            "[L200] \n",
            "[L201] Differences across the three illumination geometry groups were evaluated using the Friedman test, a non-parametric repeated-measures procedure. The test was applied separately to Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Kendall's coefficient of concordance (W) was reported as the corresponding effect-size measure.\n",
            "[L202] \n",
            "[L203] Pairwise differences between illumination geometry groups were examined using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the paired identity-level summaries. Three pairwise comparisons were conducted for each distance metric: near-frontal versus oblique, near-frontal versus large-angular, and oblique versus large-angular. Holm's step-down procedure was applied across the combined family of six pairwise tests, comprising the three Euclidean comparisons and the three cosine comparisons, to account for multiple testing.\n",
            "[L204] \n",
            "[L205] Bootstrap confidence intervals were calculated for the identity-level mean differences in the pairwise comparisons using 20,000 bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Resampling was performed at the identity level, preserving the paired structure of the repeated-measures observations. The reported intervals are 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.\n",
            "[L206] \n",
            "[L207] An exploratory comparison between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions was additionally conducted using identity-level summaries. This comparison was interpreted cautiously because the two hemispheric categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions and observations and were associated with differences in angular geometry.\n",
            "[L268] \n",
            "[L269] \n",
            "[L270] ## 3.4 Identity-level repeated-measures analysis\n",
            "[L271] \n",
            "[L272] Friedman repeated-measures tests identified differences among the three illumination geometry groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0). Each analysis included 38 identities measured across three groups.\n",
            "[L273] \n",
            "[L274] Pairwise identity-level comparisons used Wilcoxon signed-rank tests. Confidence intervals for mean paired differences were estimated using 20,000 identity-level bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Holm correction was applied across one combined family of six comparisons covering both distance measures. All six comparisons remained statistically significant.\n",
            "[L275] \n",
            "[L276] \n",
            "[L307] ## 3.6 Summary of findings\n",
            "[L308] \n",
            "[L309] The results show progressively greater reference-relative embedding displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The identity-level repeated-measures analysis identified statistically significant group differences for both distance measures, and all six pairwise comparisons remained significant following combined six-test Holm correction. The exploratory hemisphere analysis also showed higher mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions. These findings characterise embedding displacement within the evaluated dataset and fixed pretrained model; they do not establish recognition accuracy, operational drift-detection performance, or generalisation across architectures.\n",
            "[L310] \n",
            "[L311] # 4. Discussion\n",
            "[L313] ## 4.1 Principal findings\n",
            "[L314] \n",
            "[L315] This study examined reference-relative displacement in 512-dimensional FaceNet embeddings across illumination geometry conditions using a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model. Three main findings emerged. First, both Euclidean displacement and cosine distance increased progressively from near-frontal to oblique and large-angular-deviation illumination conditions. Second, the ordering of the three groups was consistent across the evaluated identities, with statistically significant repeated-measures differences for both distance measures. Third, the exploratory hemisphere comparison showed greater mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions than under frontal conditions.\n",
            "[L316] \n",
            "[L317] Across 2,128 non-reference observations, mean Euclidean displacement was 0.820496, while mean cosine distance was 0.402439. The group-level means showed a clear ordered pattern: Euclidean displacement increased from 0.401897 in the near-frontal group to 0.708976 in the oblique group and 1.118457 in the large-angular deviation group. Cosine distance followed the same ordering. These findings characterise differences in embedding position relative to an identity-specific reference; they do not directly measure successful or failed identity recognition.\n",
            "[L327] ## 4.3 Identity-level consistency and statistical evidence\n",
            "[L328] \n",
            "[L329] The Friedman tests identified differences among the three illumination groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17). Kendall's W was 1.0 for both metrics. In this analysis, W = 1.0 indicates complete agreement in the ranking of the three group conditions across the evaluated identities. It should not be interpreted as perfect recognition performance, perfect embedding quality, or detector accuracy.\n",
            "[L330] \n",
            "[L331] All six pairwise identity-level comparisons remained statistically significant after Holm correction across the combined family of six comparisons. The consistency of the ordering across identities strengthens the descriptive evidence that the observed group pattern was not confined to a small subset of identities.\n",
            "[L332] \n",
            "[L333] However, the statistical evidence should be interpreted within the study design. The descriptive statistics are based on unequal numbers of image observations across the three illumination groups (646 near-frontal, 418 oblique, and 1,064 large-angular-deviation observations). In contrast, the repeated-measures analysis uses identity-level group means from the same 38 identities across all three groups. The inferential sample therefore consists of 38 matched identities, rather than the total number of image observations. This distinction is important when interpreting the descriptive and inferential results.\n",
            "[L334] \n",
            "[L335] ## 4.4 Relationship to previous illumination research\n",
            "[L383] ## 4.8 Overall discussion\n",
            "[L384] \n",
            "[L385] Experiment 20 provides an identity-level descriptive analysis of reference-relative FaceNet embedding displacement across illumination geometry conditions in the evaluated Cropped Extended Yale B cohort. The results show an ordered increase in both distance measures from near-frontal to large-angular-deviation conditions, with consistent group rankings across the 38 evaluated identities. The exploratory hemisphere comparison showed a similar increase under beyond-frontal conditions, although its interpretation is limited by unequal condition coverage and its relationship to angular geometry.\n",
            "[L386] \n",
            "[L387] The findings contribute a focused empirical characterisation of embedding displacement under the specified dataset, reference-selection procedure, preprocessing pipeline, and pretrained model. They should be interpreted as descriptive evidence rather than proof of recognition failure, causal illumination effects, generalisation across architectures, or operational drift-detection capability.\n",
            "\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "TOPIC: wilcoxon_test\n",
            "Matching lines: 2\n",
            "[L201] Differences across the three illumination geometry groups were evaluated using the Friedman test, a non-parametric repeated-measures procedure. The test was applied separately to Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Kendall's coefficient of concordance (W) was reported as the corresponding effect-size measure.\n",
            "[L202] \n",
            "[L203] Pairwise differences between illumination geometry groups were examined using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the paired identity-level summaries. Three pairwise comparisons were conducted for each distance metric: near-frontal versus oblique, near-frontal versus large-angular, and oblique versus large-angular. Holm's step-down procedure was applied across the combined family of six pairwise tests, comprising the three Euclidean comparisons and the three cosine comparisons, to account for multiple testing.\n",
            "[L204] \n",
            "[L205] Bootstrap confidence intervals were calculated for the identity-level mean differences in the pairwise comparisons using 20,000 bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Resampling was performed at the identity level, preserving the paired structure of the repeated-measures observations. The reported intervals are 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.\n",
            "[L272] Friedman repeated-measures tests identified differences among the three illumination geometry groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0). Each analysis included 38 identities measured across three groups.\n",
            "[L273] \n",
            "[L274] Pairwise identity-level comparisons used Wilcoxon signed-rank tests. Confidence intervals for mean paired differences were estimated using 20,000 identity-level bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Holm correction was applied across one combined family of six comparisons covering both distance measures. All six comparisons remained statistically significant.\n",
            "[L275] \n",
            "[L276] \n",
            "\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "TOPIC: holm_correction\n",
            "Matching lines: 8\n",
            "[L24] tests indicated significant differences across groups for both\n",
            "[L25] metrics (chi-square = 76.0, p = 3.14e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0).\n",
            "[L26] All six pairwise comparisons remained significant following Holm\n",
            "[L27] correction. An exploratory hemisphere comparison also showed greater\n",
            "[L28] mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions.\n",
            "[L201] Differences across the three illumination geometry groups were evaluated using the Friedman test, a non-parametric repeated-measures procedure. The test was applied separately to Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Kendall's coefficient of concordance (W) was reported as the corresponding effect-size measure.\n",
            "[L202] \n",
            "[L203] Pairwise differences between illumination geometry groups were examined using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the paired identity-level summaries. Three pairwise comparisons were conducted for each distance metric: near-frontal versus oblique, near-frontal versus large-angular, and oblique versus large-angular. Holm's step-down procedure was applied across the combined family of six pairwise tests, comprising the three Euclidean comparisons and the three cosine comparisons, to account for multiple testing.\n",
            "[L204] \n",
            "[L205] Bootstrap confidence intervals were calculated for the identity-level mean differences in the pairwise comparisons using 20,000 bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Resampling was performed at the identity level, preserving the paired structure of the repeated-measures observations. The reported intervals are 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.\n",
            "[L272] Friedman repeated-measures tests identified differences among the three illumination geometry groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0). Each analysis included 38 identities measured across three groups.\n",
            "[L273] \n",
            "[L274] Pairwise identity-level comparisons used Wilcoxon signed-rank tests. Confidence intervals for mean paired differences were estimated using 20,000 identity-level bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Holm correction was applied across one combined family of six comparisons covering both distance measures. All six comparisons remained statistically significant.\n",
            "[L275] \n",
            "[L276] \n",
            "[L277] ### Table 2. Identity-level pairwise comparisons\n",
            "[L278] \n",
            "[L279] | Metric | Comparison | Mean paired difference | 95% bootstrap CI | Raw p | Six-test Holm p |\n",
            "[L280] |---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n",
            "[L281] | Euclidean displacement | Near-frontal vs Oblique | 0.307079 | [0.285544, 0.328413] | 7.276e-12 | 4.366e-11 |\n",
            "[L289] ## 3.5 Exploratory frontal and beyond-frontal comparison\n",
            "[L290] \n",
            "[L291] In the exploratory hemisphere analysis, mean Euclidean displacement was 0.706220 under frontal conditions and 1.287990 under beyond-frontal conditions. The mean paired difference (beyond-frontal minus frontal) was 0.581770 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.561986, 0.601954]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11). (Figure 3).\n",
            "[L292] \n",
            "[L293] For cosine distance, the corresponding means were 0.295816 and 0.838624. The mean paired difference was 0.542807 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.516419, 0.568516]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11). (Figure 4).\n",
            "[L294] \n",
            "[L295] ![Paired identity-level Euclidean displacement for frontal and beyond-frontal illumination hemispheres.](figures/experiment20_figure3_euclidean_hemisphere_comparison.png)\n",
            "[L307] ## 3.6 Summary of findings\n",
            "[L308] \n",
            "[L309] The results show progressively greater reference-relative embedding displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The identity-level repeated-measures analysis identified statistically significant group differences for both distance measures, and all six pairwise comparisons remained significant following combined six-test Holm correction. The exploratory hemisphere analysis also showed higher mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions. These findings characterise embedding displacement within the evaluated dataset and fixed pretrained model; they do not establish recognition accuracy, operational drift-detection performance, or generalisation across architectures.\n",
            "[L310] \n",
            "[L311] # 4. Discussion\n",
            "[L329] The Friedman tests identified differences among the three illumination groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17). Kendall's W was 1.0 for both metrics. In this analysis, W = 1.0 indicates complete agreement in the ranking of the three group conditions across the evaluated identities. It should not be interpreted as perfect recognition performance, perfect embedding quality, or detector accuracy.\n",
            "[L330] \n",
            "[L331] All six pairwise identity-level comparisons remained statistically significant after Holm correction across the combined family of six comparisons. The consistency of the ordering across identities strengthens the descriptive evidence that the observed group pattern was not confined to a small subset of identities.\n",
            "[L332] \n",
            "[L333] However, the statistical evidence should be interpreted within the study design. The descriptive statistics are based on unequal numbers of image observations across the three illumination groups (646 near-frontal, 418 oblique, and 1,064 large-angular-deviation observations). In contrast, the repeated-measures analysis uses identity-level group means from the same 38 identities across all three groups. The inferential sample therefore consists of 38 matched identities, rather than the total number of image observations. This distinction is important when interpreting the descriptive and inferential results.\n",
            "\n",
            "----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
            "TOPIC: bootstrap\n",
            "Matching lines: 5\n",
