            "[L37] ## Keywords\n",
            "[L38] \n",
            "[L39] Face recognition; Face embeddings; Illumination variation; Embedding displacement; Euclidean distance; Cosine distance\n",
            "[L40] \n",
            "[L41] # 1. Introduction\n",
            "[L49] A particularly important source of image variation is illumination. Changes in lighting direction and intensity can alter the appearance of facial regions, producing differences between images of the same individual. The effects of illumination on face recognition have been investigated in earlier research, including work examining changes in illumination direction (Moses et al., 1994).\n",
            "[L50] \n",
            "[L51] In this context, examining reference-relative embedding displacement provides an empirical way to describe how the representations of different captures of an individual vary relative to a selected reference capture. The present study applies this approach to a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model and examines Euclidean displacement and cosine distance across groups of illumination conditions.\n",
            "[L52] \n",
            "[L53] This investigation focuses on the geometry of the resulting embeddings. It does not, by itself, establish whether a face-recognition system correctly identifies individuals under those conditions.\n",
            "[L71] Despite this established body of research, there remains a need for focused empirical characterisation of how reference-relative embedding displacement varies across illumination geometry conditions at the individual-identity level within a fixed pretrained face-recognition model. In particular, comparing multiple distance measures relative to a common reference capture provides a structured way to examine whether displacement patterns differ across illumination groups and whether these differences are consistent across identities.\n",
            "[L72] \n",
            "[L73] This study addresses that specific empirical question using a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model and the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset. Rather than developing an illumination-invariant representation or evaluating identity-classification performance, the investigation examines Euclidean displacement and cosine distance between identity-specific reference embeddings and embeddings from other captures of the same individuals.\n",
            "[L74] \n",
            "[L75] The motivation is to provide a transparent, identity-level description of embedding-space variation across defined illumination geometry conditions. By examining both observation-level distributions and repeated-measures differences across identities, the study seeks to characterise the consistency and magnitude of reference-relative displacement within the evaluated dataset.\n",
            "[L81] Based on the identified research gap, this study investigates how reference-relative face embedding displacement varies across illumination geometry conditions and how consistently these differences are observed across individual identities. The investigation is guided by the following research questions:\n",
            "[L82] \n",
            "[L83] RQ1: How do Euclidean embedding displacement and cosine distance vary across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular illumination conditions?\n",
            "[L84] \n",
            "[L85] RQ2: Are the differences between illumination geometry groups consistent across evaluated identities?\n",
            "[L90] \n",
            "[L91] 1. To generate and examine fixed pretrained face embeddings for identity-matched captures under different illumination conditions.\n",
            "[L92] 2. To quantify reference-relative embedding displacement using Euclidean distance and cosine distance.\n",
            "[L93] 3. To classify common illumination conditions into near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular geometry groups.\n",
            "[L94] 4. To compare displacement measurements across illumination groups using identity-level repeated-measures statistical analysis.\n",
            "[L103] First, it provides an identity-level characterisation of reference-relative face embedding displacement across three illumination geometry groups: near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular conditions. The analysis examines how the magnitude of displacement varies across individual identities within the evaluated dataset.\n",
            "[L104] \n",
            "[L105] Second, it compares two complementary embedding-space distance measures: Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Using both measures provides a broader descriptive examination of changes in embedding representations relative to an identity-specific reference capture.\n",
            "[L106] \n",
            "[L107] Third, it applies repeated-measures statistical analysis to examine differences between the three illumination geometry groups across the evaluated identities. This analysis considers both the magnitude of group differences and the consistency of their ordering across identities.\n",
            "[L117] ## 2.1 Research Design and Experimental Overview\n",
            "[L118] \n",
            "[L119] Experiment 20 employed a descriptive empirical design to examine reference-relative displacement in deep face-recognition embeddings across illumination geometry conditions. The analysis focused on the extent to which the embedding representation of an identity's facial image differed from that identity's embedding under a designated reference illumination condition. Rather than developing or evaluating an operational drift-detection algorithm, the experiment characterised these differences using two distance measures: Euclidean displacement and cosine distance.\n",
            "[L120] \n",
            "[L121] The evaluation used the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset, comprising 2,414 standard-illumination images from 38 identities. A common-condition selection procedure identified 57 illumination conditions represented across all 38 identities, yielding 2,166 identity-condition observations. One observation per identity corresponded to the designated reference condition, A+000E+00. These 38 reference observations were used to establish the identity-specific reference embeddings. After excluding the reference condition, the primary evaluation comprised 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 illumination conditions.\n",
            "[L123] The primary unit of observation was an identity-condition pair, while identity served as the repeated-measures unit for group-level statistical comparisons. This structure allowed displacement measurements from the same 38 identities to be examined across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The observations represent identity-condition-matched captures under different illumination conditions; they are not pairs produced by applying controlled transformations to identical source images.\n",
            "[L124] \n",
            "[L125] The analysis was conducted using 512-dimensional embeddings generated by a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model with VGGFace2 weights. Each evaluation embedding was compared with the corresponding identity's reference embedding. Euclidean displacement and cosine distance were then summarised across illumination geometry groups and examined using identity-level repeated-measures statistical procedures.\n",
            "[L126] \n",
            "[L127] The study was intentionally restricted to descriptive embedding-space analysis. It did not estimate calibrated detection thresholds, operational false-alarm rates, or recognition accuracy. It also did not evaluate an independent face-recognition architecture. Consequently, the results characterise reference-relative embedding displacement within the selected dataset, preprocessing procedure, and pretrained model; they do not establish that illumination alone caused the observed differences or that the findings generalise to other recognition systems.\n",
            "[L165] Face embeddings were generated using the InceptionResnetV1 architecture implemented in facenet-pytorch, with pretrained weights associated with the VGGFace2 training dataset. The model was used as a fixed pretrained feature extractor rather than being fine-tuned on the Cropped Extended Yale B images. The same model configuration was applied throughout the Stage 2 embedding extraction procedure.\n",
            "[L166] \n",
            "[L167] The model produced a 512-dimensional embedding for each preprocessed image. Embedding extraction was performed with the model in evaluation mode, and gradient computation was disabled. These settings were used to obtain feature representations without updating model parameters during inference. The extracted embeddings were L2-normalised, providing the representations used in the subsequent reference-relative distance calculations.\n",
            "[L168] \n",
            "[L169] The recorded software environment included Python 3.13.18, PyTorch 2.14.1+cpu, torchvision 0.29.1+cpu, and facenet-pytorch 2.6.0. The embedding-generation procedure used the pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model configured for VGGFace2 weights.\n",
            "[L173] The resulting embeddings were retained with row-aligned image and identity metadata, including filenames, subject identifiers, illumination azimuths, elevations, and condition labels. These records enabled each non-reference embedding to be associated with the corresponding identity-specific reference representation during the displacement analysis.\n",
            "[L174] \n",
            "[L175] ## 2.6 Embedding Normalisation and Reference-Relative Distance Calculation\n",
            "[L176] \n",
            "[L177] The extracted 512-dimensional embeddings were L2-normalised before the reference-relative displacement analysis. For each of the 38 identities, the embedding associated with the reference illumination condition A+000E+00 served as that identity's reference representation. This produced 38 identity-specific reference embeddings.\n",
            "[L178] \n",
            "[L179] Each non-reference embedding was matched to the reference embedding belonging to the same identity. The evaluation therefore compared identity-condition observations with their corresponding within-identity reference representations rather than using a single reference vector shared across all subjects. After excluding the reference observations, the analysis included 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 common evaluation conditions.\n",
            "[L180] \n",
            "[L181] Two distance measures were used to characterise reference-relative embedding displacement. Euclidean displacement measured the L2 distance between an evaluation embedding and its corresponding identity-specific reference embedding. Cosine distance was calculated as one minus the cosine similarity between the same pair of embedding vectors. Both measures were calculated for each evaluation observation and retained alongside its identity and illumination-condition metadata. Because the embeddings were L2-normalised, the two distances are mathematically related: squared Euclidean distance equals twice cosine distance. They therefore provide related, rather than independent, measures of embedding displacement.\n",
            "[L182] \n",
            "[L183] The resulting observation-level distances were subsequently grouped by illumination geometry for descriptive and identity-level statistical analysis. The distance calculations quantify changes in embedding representation relative to the selected reference capture. They do not, by themselves, establish a change in recognition accuracy, identity verification performance, or an operational biometric drift event.\n",
            "[L197] ## 2.8 Statistical Analysis\n",
            "[L198] \n",
            "[L199] Statistical comparisons were conducted using identity-level summaries of the reference-relative distances. For each identity, the mean Euclidean displacement and mean cosine distance were calculated separately within the near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular illumination geometry groups. This aggregation provided three repeated measurements for each metric from each of the 38 identities.\n",
            "[L200] \n",
            "[L201] Differences across the three illumination geometry groups were evaluated using the Friedman test, a non-parametric repeated-measures procedure. The test was applied separately to Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Kendall's coefficient of concordance (W) was reported as the corresponding effect-size measure.\n",
            "[L202] \n",
            "[L203] Pairwise differences between illumination geometry groups were examined using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the paired identity-level summaries. Three pairwise comparisons were conducted for each distance metric: near-frontal versus oblique, near-frontal versus large-angular, and oblique versus large-angular. Holm's step-down procedure was applied across the combined family of six pairwise tests, comprising the three Euclidean comparisons and the three cosine comparisons, to account for multiple testing.\n",
            "[L204] \n",
            "[L205] Bootstrap confidence intervals were calculated for the identity-level mean differences in the pairwise comparisons using 20,000 bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Resampling was performed at the identity level, preserving the paired structure of the repeated-measures observations. The reported intervals are 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.\n",
            "[L213] A secondary exploratory analysis compared reference-relative embedding displacement between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions. The grouping was based on the angular hemisphere classification recorded in the Stage 2 analysis. The frontal category comprised 45 non-reference evaluation conditions, corresponding to 1,710 observations across the 38 identities. The beyond-frontal category comprised 11 evaluation conditions and 418 observations.\n",
            "[L214] \n",
            "[L215] For each identity, mean Euclidean displacement and mean cosine distance were calculated separately for the frontal and beyond-frontal categories. The resulting identity-level summaries were compared using paired statistical tests, with the identity serving as the unit of comparison. This approach retained the within-identity structure of the analysis.\n",
            "[L216] \n",
            "[L217] The hemisphere comparison was treated as exploratory and secondary to the three-group illumination geometry analysis. The two categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions and observations. In addition, hemisphere membership was associated with differences in angular illumination geometry. Consequently, the comparison does not isolate a hemisphere effect independently of the underlying angular distribution and should not be interpreted as evidence of a causal effect of illumination direction.\n",
            "[L242] ## 3.2 Overall reference-relative embedding displacement\n",
            "[L243] \n",
            "[L244] Across 2,128 evaluation observations, mean Euclidean displacement was 0.820496 (SD = 0.362940; median = 0.817311), with values ranging from 0.151681 to 1.522514. Mean cosine distance was 0.402439 (SD = 0.309758; median = 0.333999), ranging from 0.011504 to 1.159025.\n",
            "[L245] \n",
            "[L246] \n",
            "[L247] ## 3.3 Displacement across illumination geometry groups\n",
            "[L248] \n",
            "[L249] Both distance measures increased progressively across the illumination geometry groups. Mean Euclidean displacement increased from 0.401897 under near-frontal conditions to 0.708976 under oblique conditions and 1.118457 under large-angular deviation conditions. Mean cosine distance followed the same ordering, increasing from 0.086877 to 0.262274 and 0.649095, respectively. The identity-level patterns are shown in Figures 1 and 2.\n",
            "[L250] \n",
            "[L251] \n",
            "[L252] ### Table 1. Descriptive statistics by illumination geometry group\n",
            "[L253] \n",
            "[L254] | Illumination group | Observations | Identities | Conditions | Euclidean mean | Euclidean SD | Cosine mean | Cosine SD |\n",
            "[L255] |---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n",
            "[L256] | Near-frontal | 646 | 38 | 17 | 0.4019 | 0.1107 | 0.0869 | 0.0479 |\n",
            "[L259] \n",
            "[L260] \n",
