            "Line 201: Differences across the three illumination geometry groups were evaluated using the Friedman test, a non-parametric repeated-measures procedure. The test was applied separately to Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Kendall's coefficient of concordance (W) was reported as the corresponding effect-size measure.\n",
            "Line 203: Pairwise differences between illumination geometry groups were examined using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the paired identity-level summaries. Three pairwise comparisons were conducted for each distance metric: near-frontal versus oblique, near-frontal versus large-angular, and oblique versus large-angular. Holm's step-down procedure was applied across the combined family of six pairwise tests, comprising the three Euclidean comparisons and the three cosine comparisons, to account for multiple testing.\n",
            "Line 205: Bootstrap confidence intervals were calculated for the identity-level mean differences in the pairwise comparisons using 20,000 bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Resampling was performed at the identity level, preserving the paired structure of the repeated-measures observations. The reported intervals are 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.\n",
            "Line 211: ## 2.9 Exploratory Hemisphere-Based Analysis\n",
            "Line 213: A secondary exploratory analysis compared reference-relative embedding displacement between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions. The grouping was based on the angular hemisphere classification recorded in the Stage 2 analysis. The frontal category comprised 45 non-reference evaluation conditions, corresponding to 1,710 observations across the 38 identities. The beyond-frontal category comprised 11 evaluation conditions and 418 observations.\n",
            "Line 215: For each identity, mean Euclidean displacement and mean cosine distance were calculated separately for the frontal and beyond-frontal categories. The resulting identity-level summaries were compared using paired statistical tests, with the identity serving as the unit of comparison. This approach retained the within-identity structure of the analysis.\n",
            "Line 217: The hemisphere comparison was treated as exploratory and secondary to the three-group illumination geometry analysis. The two categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions and observations. In addition, hemisphere membership was associated with differences in angular illumination geometry. Consequently, the comparison does not isolate a hemisphere effect independently of the underlying angular distribution and should not be interpreted as evidence of a causal effect of illumination direction.\n",
            "Line 272: Friedman repeated-measures tests identified differences among the three illumination geometry groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0). Each analysis included 38 identities measured across three groups.\n",
            "Line 274: Pairwise identity-level comparisons used Wilcoxon signed-rank tests. Confidence intervals for mean paired differences were estimated using 20,000 identity-level bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Holm correction was applied across one combined family of six comparisons covering both distance measures. All six comparisons remained statistically significant.\n",
            "Line 279: | Metric | Comparison | Mean paired difference | 95% bootstrap CI | Raw p | Six-test Holm p |\n",
            "Line 291: In the exploratory hemisphere analysis, mean Euclidean displacement was 0.706220 under frontal conditions and 1.287990 under beyond-frontal conditions. The mean paired difference (beyond-frontal minus frontal) was 0.581770 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.561986, 0.601954]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11). (Figure 3).\n",
            "Line 293: For cosine distance, the corresponding means were 0.295816 and 0.838624. The mean paired difference was 0.542807 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.516419, 0.568516]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11). (Figure 4).\n",
            "Line 295: ![Paired identity-level Euclidean displacement for frontal and beyond-frontal illumination hemispheres.](figures/experiment20_figure3_euclidean_hemisphere_comparison.png)\n",
            "Line 297: *Figure 3. Paired identity-level Euclidean displacement by illumination hemisphere. Each thin line connects the frontal and beyond-frontal mean displacement for one identity; the thick olive line represents the mean across the 38 identities. This comparison is exploratory.*\n",
            "Line 299: ![Paired identity-level cosine distance for frontal and beyond-frontal illumination hemispheres.](figures/experiment20_figure4_cosine_hemisphere_comparison.png)\n",
            "Line 301: *Figure 4. Paired identity-level cosine distance by illumination hemisphere. Each thin line connects the frontal and beyond-frontal mean distance for one identity; the thick olive line represents the mean across the 38 identities. This comparison is exploratory.*\n",
            "Line 304: This comparison is exploratory. The frontal and beyond-frontal categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions, and hemisphere membership is related to angular illumination geometry. Therefore, the observed differences should not be interpreted as an isolated causal effect of hemisphere direction.\n",
            "Line 309: The results show progressively greater reference-relative embedding displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The identity-level repeated-measures analysis identified statistically significant group differences for both distance measures, and all six pairwise comparisons remained significant following combined six-test Holm correction. The exploratory hemisphere analysis also showed higher mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions. These findings characterise embedding displacement within the evaluated dataset and fixed pretrained model; they do not establish recognition accuracy, operational drift-detection performance, or generalisation across architectures.\n",
            "Line 315: This study examined reference-relative displacement in 512-dimensional FaceNet embeddings across illumination geometry conditions using a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model. Three main findings emerged. First, both Euclidean displacement and cosine distance increased progressively from near-frontal to oblique and large-angular-deviation illumination conditions. Second, the ordering of the three groups was consistent across the evaluated identities, with statistically significant repeated-measures differences for both distance measures. Third, the exploratory hemisphere comparison showed greater mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions than under frontal conditions.\n",
            "Line 329: The Friedman tests identified differences among the three illumination groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17). Kendall's W was 1.0 for both metrics. In this analysis, W = 1.0 indicates complete agreement in the ranking of the three group conditions across the evaluated identities. It should not be interpreted as perfect recognition performance, perfect embedding quality, or detector accuracy.\n",
            "Line 331: All six pairwise identity-level comparisons remained statistically significant after Holm correction across the combined family of six comparisons. The consistency of the ordering across identities strengthens the descriptive evidence that the observed group pattern was not confined to a small subset of identities.\n",
            "Line 369: **Exploratory hemisphere comparison.** The frontal and beyond-frontal categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions. Hemisphere membership is also related to angular geometry. The hemisphere comparison is consequently exploratory and does not isolate an independent hemisphere effect.\n",
            "Line 385: Experiment 20 provides an identity-level descriptive analysis of reference-relative FaceNet embedding displacement across illumination geometry conditions in the evaluated Cropped Extended Yale B cohort. The results show an ordered increase in both distance measures from near-frontal to large-angular-deviation conditions, with consistent group rankings across the 38 evaluated identities. The exploratory hemisphere comparison showed a similar increase under beyond-frontal conditions, although its interpretation is limited by unequal condition coverage and its relationship to angular geometry.\n",
