            "\n",
            "5. INDEPENDENCE AND STATISTICAL LIMITATION LANGUAGE\n",
            "Line 1: # Reference-Relative Face Embedding Displacement Across Illumination Geometry Conditions: An Identity-Level Empirical Study\n",
            "Line 11: comprising 38 identities and 2,414 images. A common reference capture\n",
            "Line 27: correction. An exploratory hemisphere comparison also showed greater\n",
            "Line 75: The motivation is to provide a transparent, identity-level description of embedding-space variation across defined illumination geometry conditions. By examining both observation-level distributions and repeated-measures differences across identities, the study seeks to characterise the consistency and magnitude of reference-relative displacement within the evaluated dataset.\n",
            "Line 94: 4. To compare displacement measurements across illumination groups using identity-level repeated-measures statistical analysis.\n",
            "Line 97: The first two research questions form the primary analytical focus of the study. The third question provides an additional exploratory examination of illumination geometry. The statistical hypotheses associated with these comparisons were formulated after the experimental analyses and are therefore treated as retrospective analytical expectations rather than preregistered hypotheses.\n",
            "Line 103: First, it provides an identity-level characterisation of reference-relative face embedding displacement across three illumination geometry groups: near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular conditions. The analysis examines how the magnitude of displacement varies across individual identities within the evaluated dataset.\n",
            "Line 107: Third, it applies repeated-measures statistical analysis to examine differences between the three illumination geometry groups across the evaluated identities. This analysis considers both the magnitude of group differences and the consistency of their ordering across identities.\n",
            "Line 109: Fourth, it provides an exploratory comparison of frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions, examining whether reference-relative displacement differs between these broader geometric categories.\n",
            "Line 111: The scope of the study is deliberately limited. The evaluation uses the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset, comprising 38 identities and 2,414 standard-illumination images, and a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model. Of the 2,414 standard-illumination images, 2,166 observations belong to the 57 common illumination conditions. After excluding the reference condition, 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 evaluation conditions are included in the displacement analysis. Each identity is represented by one reference capture, against which embeddings from other captures are compared. The evaluation therefore examines different identity-matched captures rather than exact transformations of identical source images.\n",
            "Line 113: The study does not evaluate face-recognition accuracy, develop an illumination-invariant representation, establish operational drift-detection capability, or validate generalisation across independent recognition architectures. Its findings should be interpreted as an empirical characterisation of reference-relative embedding displacement under the specific dataset, preprocessing procedure, and model used in this investigation.\n",
            "Line 121: The evaluation used the Cropped Extended Yale B dataset, comprising 2,414 standard-illumination images from 38 identities. A common-condition selection procedure identified 57 illumination conditions represented across all 38 identities, yielding 2,166 identity-condition observations. One observation per identity corresponded to the designated reference condition, A+000E+00. These 38 reference observations were used to establish the identity-specific reference embeddings. After excluding the reference condition, the primary evaluation comprised 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 illumination conditions.\n",
            "Line 123: The primary unit of observation was an identity-condition pair, while identity served as the repeated-measures unit for group-level statistical comparisons. This structure allowed displacement measurements from the same 38 identities to be examined across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The observations represent identity-condition-matched captures under different illumination conditions; they are not pairs produced by applying controlled transformations to identical source images.\n",
            "Line 125: The analysis was conducted using 512-dimensional embeddings generated by a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model with VGGFace2 weights. Each evaluation embedding was compared with the corresponding identity's reference embedding. Euclidean displacement and cosine distance were then summarised across illumination geometry groups and examined using identity-level repeated-measures statistical procedures.\n",
            "Line 127: The study was intentionally restricted to descriptive embedding-space analysis. It did not estimate calibrated detection thresholds, operational false-alarm rates, or recognition accuracy. It also did not evaluate an independent face-recognition architecture. Consequently, the results characterise reference-relative embedding displacement within the selected dataset, preprocessing procedure, and pretrained model; they do not establish that illumination alone caused the observed differences or that the findings generalise to other recognition systems.\n",
            "Line 131: The external evaluation dataset used in Experiment 20 was the Cropped Extended Yale B frontal-pose illumination subset. It contains cropped grayscale facial images captured under different illumination conditions for 38 identities. This subset should be distinguished from the complete multi-pose Extended Yale B dataset.\n",
            "Line 133: The dataset archive was acquired through a community-hosted Academic Torrents listing. The acquisition record identifies the source listing and records an archive size of 58,493,820 bytes. The downloaded ZIP archive passed its integrity test and contained 2,586 members. Its SHA-256 checksum was recorded as 8bbabf3836d077d60626c9ee3c54c2d2851a8d2c15c8b7e13b9bc162e86ea573. The archive provenance remains subject to an important limitation: it was not independently authenticated against an official UCSD checksum. Accordingly, the acquisition record and checksum provide traceability for the downloaded archive but do not establish official dataset authenticity.\n",
            "Line 135: The archive contained 2,452 PGM image files, all of which were successfully decoded during the image-integrity audit. Filename and condition validation identified 2,414 standard-illumination images and 38 ambient reference images. The ambient images were excluded from the standard-illumination analysis. The retained standard-illumination images represented 64 distinct illumination conditions across the 38 identities. Seven identities did not contain every condition in the overall set.\n",
            "Line 137: To support comparisons with complete identity coverage, the analysis used the 57 illumination conditions shared by all 38 identities. This common-condition subset comprised 2,166 identity-condition observations. The remaining conditions that were not shared by every identity were not included in analyses requiring complete identity-condition coverage. One observation per identity corresponded to the reference condition, A+000E+00; after its exclusion, 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 conditions formed the primary evaluation cohort.\n",
            "Line 143: The subject cohort comprised 38 identities represented in the Cropped Extended Yale B frontal-pose illumination subset. The standard-illumination image collection contained 64 distinct illumination conditions across the cohort; however, not every condition was available for every identity. Seven identities had incomplete condition coverage. To preserve a consistent identity set across the primary condition-based comparisons, the analysis retained only conditions represented for all 38 identities.\n",
            "Line 145: This complete-case condition selection produced 57 common illumination conditions and 2,166 identity-condition observations, corresponding to 38 observations for each condition. The selection was based on shared condition availability rather than on embedding displacement values or statistical outcomes. Conditions not represented across all 38 identities were excluded from analyses requiring complete identity-condition coverage.\n",
            "Line 147: The condition A+000E+00 was designated as the reference condition. One reference observation was available for each of the 38 identities, providing an identity-specific reference embedding. The reference observations were retained for calculating reference-relative distances but excluded from the non-reference evaluation set. Consequently, the primary evaluation comprised the remaining 56 common conditions and 2,128 identity-condition observations.\n",
            "Line 151: The geometry groups represent the angular deviation of the illumination direction relative to the camera axis, as defined by the study's condition-grouping procedure. They do not represent directly measured illumination intensity. The same 38 identities were represented in each of the three evaluation groups, enabling identity-level repeated-measures comparisons. The resulting observations are identity-condition-matched captures under different lighting conditions, not exact image-level pairs generated from a single source image.\n",
            "Line 171: Although the model architecture, pretrained training source, software versions, output dimensionality, and inference settings were recorded, the exact pretrained weight-file identity and its SHA256 checksum were not preserved. Consequently, the model configuration is documented at the architecture and pretrained-source level, but exact weight-file reproducibility cannot be independently established from the retained records.\n",
            "Line 177: The extracted 512-dimensional embeddings were L2-normalised before the reference-relative displacement analysis. For each of the 38 identities, the embedding associated with the reference illumination condition A+000E+00 served as that identity's reference representation. This produced 38 identity-specific reference embeddings.\n",
            "Line 181: Two distance measures were used to characterise reference-relative embedding displacement. Euclidean displacement measured the L2 distance between an evaluation embedding and its corresponding identity-specific reference embedding. Cosine distance was calculated as one minus the cosine similarity between the same pair of embedding vectors. Both measures were calculated for each evaluation observation and retained alongside its identity and illumination-condition metadata. Because the embeddings were L2-normalised, the two distances are mathematically related: squared Euclidean distance equals twice cosine distance. They therefore provide related, rather than independent, measures of embedding displacement.\n",
            "Line 183: The resulting observation-level distances were subsequently grouped by illumination geometry for descriptive and identity-level statistical analysis. The distance calculations quantify changes in embedding representation relative to the selected reference capture. They do not, by themselves, establish a change in recognition accuracy, identity verification performance, or an operational biometric drift event.\n",
            "Line 193: Across the 56 non-reference evaluation conditions, 17 conditions were classified as near-frontal, 11 as oblique, and 28 as large-angular. Across the 38 identities, these groups contained 646, 418, and 1,064 evaluation observations, respectively. All three groups therefore included observations from the same 38 identities, supporting the repeated-measures comparisons conducted at the identity level.\n",
            "Line 199: Statistical comparisons were conducted using identity-level summaries of the reference-relative distances. For each identity, the mean Euclidean displacement and mean cosine distance were calculated separately within the near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular illumination geometry groups. This aggregation provided three repeated measurements for each metric from each of the 38 identities.\n",
            "Line 201: Differences across the three illumination geometry groups were evaluated using the Friedman test, a non-parametric repeated-measures procedure. The test was applied separately to Euclidean displacement and cosine distance. Kendall's coefficient of concordance (W) was reported as the corresponding effect-size measure.\n",
            "Line 203: Pairwise differences between illumination geometry groups were examined using Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the paired identity-level summaries. Three pairwise comparisons were conducted for each distance metric: near-frontal versus oblique, near-frontal versus large-angular, and oblique versus large-angular. Holm's step-down procedure was applied across the combined family of six pairwise tests, comprising the three Euclidean comparisons and the three cosine comparisons, to account for multiple testing.\n",
            "Line 205: As a sensitivity analysis, exact two-sided paired sign tests were additionally conducted on the identity-level mean differences for each of the six primary pairwise comparisons. The sign tests evaluated whether the direction of the paired differences was consistently positive or negative across identities, without relying on the symmetry assumption associated with the Wilcoxon signed-rank test. Tied differences, if present, were excluded from the sign-test calculation. Holm's step-down correction was applied across the same combined family of six primary sign tests. These analyses were used to assess the robustness of the directional findings and did not replace the primary Wilcoxon signed-rank tests.\n",
            "Line 207: Bootstrap confidence intervals were calculated for the identity-level mean differences in the pairwise comparisons using 20,000 bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Resampling was performed at the identity level, preserving the paired structure of the repeated-measures observations. The reported intervals are 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.\n",
            "Line 209: An exploratory comparison between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions was additionally conducted using identity-level summaries. This comparison was interpreted cautiously because the two hemispheric categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions and observations and were associated with differences in angular geometry.\n",
            "Line 213: ## 2.9 Exploratory Hemisphere-Based Analysis\n",
            "Line 215: A secondary exploratory analysis compared reference-relative embedding displacement between frontal and beyond-frontal illumination conditions. The grouping was based on the angular hemisphere classification recorded in the Stage 2 analysis. The frontal category comprised 45 non-reference evaluation conditions, corresponding to 1,710 observations across the 38 identities. The beyond-frontal category comprised 11 evaluation conditions and 418 observations.\n",
            "Line 217: For each identity, mean Euclidean displacement and mean cosine distance were calculated separately for the frontal and beyond-frontal categories. The resulting identity-level summaries were compared using paired statistical tests, with the identity serving as the unit of comparison. This approach retained the within-identity structure of the analysis.\n",
            "Line 219: As an additional sensitivity analysis, exact two-sided paired sign tests were performed separately for Euclidean displacement and cosine distance using the identity-level differences between beyond-frontal and frontal conditions. Holm correction was applied across these two hemisphere sign tests as a separate exploratory correction family. This correction family was kept separate from the six primary illumination-geometry comparisons because the hemisphere analysis was secondary and exploratory.\n",
            "Line 221: The hemisphere comparison was treated as exploratory and secondary to the three-group illumination geometry analysis. The two categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions and observations. In addition, hemisphere membership was associated with differences in angular illumination geometry. Consequently, the comparison does not isolate a hemisphere effect independently of the underlying angular distribution and should not be interpreted as evidence of a causal effect of illumination direction.\n",
            "Line 227: Several methodological limitations should be considered when interpreting the findings and assessing reproducibility. First, the Cropped Extended Yale B subset was obtained through a community-hosted Academic Torrents listing. Although the acquired archive and frozen dataset were recorded using SHA256 checksums and integrity checks, the source was not independently authenticated against an official UCSD checksum. The dataset should therefore be described according to its documented acquisition provenance rather than as an independently authenticated official release.\n",
            "Line 229: Second, the exact pretrained InceptionResnetV1 weight-file identity and its SHA256 checksum were not preserved. The architecture, VGGFace2 pretrained source, software versions, and inference configuration were recorded, but these records do not establish that an independent reproduction would load byte-identical model weights.\n",
            "Line 233: Fourth, the selected cropped images were processed using direct resizing without MTCNN face detection or facial alignment. This procedure differs from the MTCNN-based preprocessing used in Experiment 18, limiting direct comparability between the two experimental stages. The Stage 2 evaluation also used a single pretrained architecture and did not include independent architecture validation.\n",
            "Line 241: The source dataset contained 2,414 standard-illumination images from 38 identities. Of these, 2,166 observations belonged to 57 common illumination conditions. One reference capture was selected for each identity using condition A+000E+00. After excluding the 38 reference observations, the evaluation comprised 2,128 non-reference observations across 56 common evaluation conditions. All 38 identities were represented in the evaluation.\n",
            "Line 253: Both distance measures increased progressively across the illumination geometry groups. Mean Euclidean displacement increased from 0.401897 under near-frontal conditions to 0.708976 under oblique conditions and 1.118457 under large-angular deviation conditions. Mean cosine distance followed the same ordering, increasing from 0.086877 to 0.262274 and 0.649095, respectively. The identity-level patterns are shown in Figures 1 and 2.\n",
            "Line 265: ![Identity-level Euclidean displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups.](figures/experiment20_figure1_euclidean_illumination_groups.png)\n",
            "Line 267: *Figure 1. Identity-level Euclidean displacement across illumination geometry groups. Thin lines represent the mean displacement for each of the 38 identities within each group; the thick olive line represents the mean across identities.*\n",
            "Line 269: ![Identity-level cosine distance across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups.](figures/experiment20_figure2_cosine_illumination_groups.png)\n",
            "Line 271: *Figure 2. Identity-level cosine distance across illumination geometry groups. Thin lines represent the mean distance for each of the 38 identities within each group; the thick olive line represents the mean across identities.*\n",
            "Line 274: ## 3.4 Identity-level repeated-measures analysis\n",
            "Line 276: Friedman repeated-measures tests identified differences among the three illumination geometry groups for Euclidean displacement (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0) and cosine distance (χ²(2) = 76.0, p = 3.139e-17, Kendall's W = 1.0). Each analysis included 38 identities measured across three groups.\n",
            "Line 278: Pairwise identity-level comparisons used Wilcoxon signed-rank tests. Confidence intervals for mean paired differences were estimated using 20,000 identity-level bootstrap iterations with random seed 2026274. Holm correction was applied across one combined family of six comparisons covering both distance measures. All six comparisons remained statistically significant.\n",
            "Line 280: In the sign-test sensitivity analysis, all 38 identities showed positive paired differences for each of the six primary comparisons, with no negative differences or ties. The exact two-sided sign-test p-value was 7.276 × 10⁻¹² for each comparison. Following Holm correction across the six-test family, all adjusted p-values were 4.366 × 10⁻¹¹. This consistent direction of differences provides sensitivity evidence supporting the ordering observed in the primary Wilcoxon analysis.\n",
            "Line 283: ### Table 2. Identity-level pairwise comparisons\n",
            "Line 295: ## 3.5 Exploratory frontal and beyond-frontal comparison\n",
            "Line 297: In the exploratory hemisphere analysis, mean Euclidean displacement was 0.706220 under frontal conditions and 1.287990 under beyond-frontal conditions. The mean paired difference (beyond-frontal minus frontal) was 0.581770 (95% bootstrap CI: [0.561986, 0.601954]; Holm-adjusted p = 1.455e-11). (Figure 3).\n",
            "Line 301: In the exploratory hemisphere sign-test sensitivity analysis, all 38 identities showed positive differences between beyond-frontal and frontal conditions for both Euclidean displacement and cosine distance, with no negative differences or ties. The exact two-sided sign-test p-value was 7.276 × 10⁻¹² for each metric. Following Holm correction across the separate two-test hemisphere family, both adjusted p-values were 1.455 × 10⁻¹¹. These results provide additional evidence of directional consistency but do not establish an independent or causal hemisphere effect.\n",
            "Line 303: ![Paired identity-level Euclidean displacement for frontal and beyond-frontal illumination hemispheres.](figures/experiment20_figure3_euclidean_hemisphere_comparison.png)\n",
            "Line 305: *Figure 3. Paired identity-level Euclidean displacement by illumination hemisphere. Each thin line connects the frontal and beyond-frontal mean displacement for one identity; the thick olive line represents the mean across the 38 identities. This comparison is exploratory.*\n",
            "Line 307: ![Paired identity-level cosine distance for frontal and beyond-frontal illumination hemispheres.](figures/experiment20_figure4_cosine_hemisphere_comparison.png)\n",
            "Line 309: *Figure 4. Paired identity-level cosine distance by illumination hemisphere. Each thin line connects the frontal and beyond-frontal mean distance for one identity; the thick olive line represents the mean across the 38 identities. This comparison is exploratory.*\n",
            "Line 312: This comparison is exploratory. The frontal and beyond-frontal categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions, and hemisphere membership is related to angular illumination geometry. Therefore, the observed differences should not be interpreted as an isolated causal effect of hemisphere direction.\n",
            "Line 317: The results show progressively greater reference-relative embedding displacement across near-frontal, oblique, and large-angular-deviation illumination groups. The identity-level repeated-measures analysis identified statistically significant group differences for both distance measures, and all six pairwise comparisons remained significant following combined six-test Holm correction. The exploratory hemisphere analysis also showed higher mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions. These findings characterise embedding displacement within the evaluated dataset and fixed pretrained model; they do not establish recognition accuracy, operational drift-detection performance, or generalisation across architectures.\n",
            "Line 323: This study examined reference-relative displacement in 512-dimensional FaceNet embeddings across illumination geometry conditions using a fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model. Three main findings emerged. First, both Euclidean displacement and cosine distance increased progressively from near-frontal to oblique and large-angular-deviation illumination conditions. Second, the ordering of the three groups was consistent across the evaluated identities, with statistically significant repeated-measures differences for both distance measures. Third, the exploratory hemisphere comparison showed greater mean displacement under beyond-frontal conditions than under frontal conditions.\n",
            "Line 335: ## 4.3 Identity-level consistency and statistical evidence\n",
            "Line 339: All six pairwise identity-level comparisons remained statistically significant after Holm correction across the combined family of six comparisons. The consistency of the ordering across identities strengthens the descriptive evidence that the observed group pattern was not confined to a small subset of identities.\n",
            "Line 341: However, the statistical evidence should be interpreted within the study design. The descriptive statistics are based on unequal numbers of image observations across the three illumination groups (646 near-frontal, 418 oblique, and 1,064 large-angular-deviation observations). In contrast, the repeated-measures analysis uses identity-level group means from the same 38 identities across all three groups. The inferential sample therefore consists of 38 matched identities, rather than the total number of image observations. This distinction is important when interpreting the descriptive and inferential results.\n",
            "Line 355: These measures are not independent forms of validation in this experiment. Because the embeddings were L2-normalised, squared Euclidean distance is mathematically related to cosine distance: squared Euclidean distance equals twice the cosine distance. The parallel trends in the two measures should therefore not be presented as two independent confirmations of the same finding.\n",
            "Line 369: **Dataset coverage and provenance.** The evaluation used a cropped frontal-pose subset of Extended Yale B, rather than the complete multi-pose dataset. The acquired archive was obtained through a community-hosted Academic Torrents listing and was not independently authenticated against an official dataset checksum. These factors should be considered when assessing reproducibility and external validity.\n",
            "Line 377: **Exploratory hemisphere comparison.** The frontal and beyond-frontal categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions. Hemisphere membership is also related to angular geometry. The hemisphere comparison is consequently exploratory and does not isolate an independent hemisphere effect.\n",
            "Line 379: **Related distance measures.** Euclidean displacement and cosine distance are mathematically related for L2-normalised embeddings. Their agreement does not constitute independent validation using unrelated measurement constructs.\n",
            "Line 383: Future work should first investigate illumination variation using controlled transformations of identical source images. Such a design would help separate illumination effects from differences between independent captures. Multiple reference captures per identity could also be examined to assess the sensitivity of reference-relative displacement to reference selection.\n",
            "Line 387: A further extension would connect embedding displacement to recognition outcomes. Genuine and impostor comparisons, verification metrics, and identity-level performance analysis would be necessary to determine whether larger displacement corresponds to a measurable reduction in recognition performance.\n",
            "Line 393: Experiment 20 provides an identity-level descriptive analysis of reference-relative FaceNet embedding displacement across illumination geometry conditions in the evaluated Cropped Extended Yale B cohort. The results show an ordered increase in both distance measures from near-frontal to large-angular-deviation conditions, with consistent group rankings across the 38 evaluated identities. The exploratory hemisphere comparison showed a similar increase under beyond-frontal conditions, although its interpretation is limited by unequal condition coverage and its relationship to angular geometry.\n",
            "\n",
            "6. FULL LIMITATIONS SECTION\n",
            "359: ## 4.6 Limitations\n",
            "360: \n",
            "361: Several limitations define the scope of these findings.\n",
            "362: \n",
            "363: **Different image captures.** The evaluation compares different captures of the same identities under different illumination conditions. These are not exact transformations of identical source images. Other capture-related differences may contribute to the observed displacement, preventing exclusive attribution to illumination.\n",
            "364: \n",
            "365: **Single reference capture.** Each identity was represented by one reference embedding. This design provides a common reference for the descriptive comparisons, but it does not estimate within-identity reference variability or the distribution of possible reference embeddings.\n",
            "366: \n",
            "367: **Single recognition architecture.** Embeddings were generated using one fixed pretrained InceptionResnetV1 model trained on VGGFace2. The results cannot establish whether the same displacement pattern occurs in other architectures or embedding models.\n",
            "368: \n",
            "369: **Dataset coverage and provenance.** The evaluation used a cropped frontal-pose subset of Extended Yale B, rather than the complete multi-pose dataset. The acquired archive was obtained through a community-hosted Academic Torrents listing and was not independently authenticated against an official dataset checksum. These factors should be considered when assessing reproducibility and external validity.\n",
            "370: \n",
            "371: **Preprocessing and alignment.** The evaluation used the documented direct-resize preprocessing pipeline. A full-cohort face-alignment procedure was not established for this dataset. The results should therefore be understood in the context of the specific preprocessing pipeline used in this experiment.\n",
            "372: \n",
            "373: **No recognition-performance evaluation.** The study did not measure identification accuracy, verification accuracy, false acceptance, false rejection, or recognition performance under operational conditions. Embedding displacement cannot be substituted for these outcomes.\n",
            "374: \n",
            "375: **No operational drift detection.** The experiment did not calibrate detection thresholds, estimate operational false-alarm rates, or evaluate a streaming drift detection method. The findings are descriptive and should not be interpreted as evidence of deployment readiness.\n",
            "376: \n",
            "377: **Exploratory hemisphere comparison.** The frontal and beyond-frontal categories contained unequal numbers of illumination conditions. Hemisphere membership is also related to angular geometry. The hemisphere comparison is consequently exploratory and does not isolate an independent hemisphere effect.\n",
            "378: \n",
            "379: **Related distance measures.** Euclidean displacement and cosine distance are mathematically related for L2-normalised embeddings. Their agreement does not constitute independent validation using unrelated measurement constructs.\n",
            "380: \n",
            "\n",
